2025-12-18T10:44:29.008Z [iterm2] [info] Message from server: {"jsonrpc":"2.0","id":33,"result":{"content":[{"type":"text","text":"{\n  \"success\": true,\n  \"file_path\": \"/Users/happyhawking/Documents/Projects/Current/writer/transcripts/sample_interview.txt\",\n  \"content\": \"Interview with Sergeant James \\\"Jim\\\" Mitchell\\n6th Armored Division, Company B, 68th Tank Battalion\\nInterviewed on March 15, 1995\\n\\nInterviewer: Thank you for agreeing to share your story, Sergeant Mitchell. Can you start by telling us about your early days in the service?\\n\\nJim Mitchell: Well, I enlisted in August of 1942, right after I turned 19. I was from a small town in Ohio, and most of my buddies were signing up. They sent me to Camp Hood in Texas for basic training. That was in September of '42. Hot as blazes down there, I tell you. We trained on the M4 Sherman tanks for months.\\n\\nInterviewer: What was training like at Camp Hood?\\n\\nJim Mitchell: It was tough but necessary. We learned everything about those Shermans - how to drive them, shoot from them, maintain them. The drill sergeants were hard on us, but they knew what we'd be facing. I remember this one sergeant, Sergeant Kowalski, he'd make us do drills until we could load and fire in our sleep. Camp Hood was massive - thousands of us training there. We left for Camp Polk in Louisiana in early 1943 for more advanced training.\\n\\nInterviewer: When did you ship out to Europe?\\n\\nJim Mitchell: We deployed in February of 1944. Took a ship from New York, spent about two weeks crossing the Atlantic. Got to England first, then eventually made our way to France after D-Day. I wasn't in the initial D-Day landing, but we came in shortly after, around late July 1944.\\n\\nInterviewer: What was your first major engagement?\\n\\nJim Mitchell: That would be the Brittany Campaign in August of '44. We were tasked with clearing out German positions around Brest. That was brutal. The Germans had fortified the city heavily. I remember rolling into the outskirts of Brest, and the artillery fire was constant. We lost a lot of good men there. My gunner, Private Tommy Walsh from Brooklyn, he got hit by shrapnel during one of the bombardments. Tore up his left arm pretty bad.\\n\\nInterviewer: Can you tell us more about the fighting in Brittany?\\n\\nJim Mitchell: The whole Brittany peninsula was a fight. After Brest, we moved toward Rennes. The hedgerows made tank operations difficult. You couldn't see what was around the corner. The Germans had panzerfausts, and they'd hide in the hedgerows and ambush us. We developed tactics - infantry would clear ahead of us, we'd provide covering fire. It was slow going, but we made progress.\\n\\nInterviewer: What happened after Brittany?\\n\\nJim Mitchell: After securing the area, we moved north. Participated in pushing toward the German border. That fall of 1944 was a blur of small towns and villages. Somewhere in eastern France, I can't remember the exact name, we liberated a small town. The people were so grateful. An old woman gave me a bottle of wine she'd hidden from the Germans. Still have it, never opened it.\\n\\nInterviewer: Were you involved in the Battle of the Bulge?\\n\\n\",\n  \"file_size_bytes\": 5753,\n  \"content_length\": 2875\n}"}],"structuredContent":{"result":"{\n  \"success\": true,\n  \"file_path\": \"/Users/happyhawking/Documents/Projects/Current/writer/transcripts/sample_interview.txt\",\n  \"content\": \"Interview with Sergeant James \\\"Jim\\\" Mitchell\\n6th Armored Division, Company B, 68th Tank Battalion\\nInterviewed on March 15, 1995\\n\\nInterviewer: Thank you for agreeing to share your story, Sergeant Mitchell. Can you start by telling us about your early days in the service?\\n\\nJim Mitchell: Well, I enlisted in August of 1942, right after I turned 19. I was from a small town in Ohio, and most of my buddies were signing up. They sent me to Camp Hood in Texas for basic training. That was in September of '42. Hot as blazes down there, I tell you. We trained on the M4 Sherman tanks for months.\\n\\nInterviewer: What was training like at Camp Hood?\\n\\nJim Mitchell: It was tough but necessary. We learned everything about those Shermans - how to drive them, shoot from them, maintain them. The drill sergeants were hard on us, but they knew what we'd be facing. I remember this one sergeant, Sergeant Kowalski, he'd make us do drills until we could load and fire in our sleep. Camp Hood was massive - thousands of us training there. We left for Camp Polk in Louisiana in early 1943 for more advanced training.\\n\\nInterviewer: When did you ship out to Europe?\\n\\nJim Mitchell: We deployed in February of 1944. Took a ship from New York, spent about two weeks crossing the Atlantic. Got to England first, then eventually made our way to France after D-Day. I wasn't in the initial D-Day landing, but we came in shortly after, around late July 1944.\\n\\nInterviewer: What was your first major engagement?\\n\\nJim Mitchell: That would be the Brittany Campaign in August of '44. We were tasked with clearing out German positions around Brest. That was brutal. The Germans had fortified the city heavily. I remember rolling into the outskirts of Brest, and the artillery fire was constant. We lost a lot of good men there. My gunner, Private Tommy Walsh from Brooklyn, he got hit by shrapnel during one of the bombardments. Tore up his left arm pretty bad.\\n\\nInterviewer: Can you tell us more about the fighting in Brittany?\\n\\nJim Mitchell: The whole Brittany peninsula was a fight. After Brest, we moved toward Rennes. The hedgerows made tank operations difficult. You couldn't see what was around the corner. The Germans had panzerfausts, and they'd hide in the hedgerows and ambush us. We developed tactics - infantry would clear ahead of us, we'd provide covering fire. It was slow going, but we made progress.\\n\\nInterviewer: What happened after Brittany?\\n\\nJim Mitchell: After securing the area, we moved north. Participated in pushing toward the German border. That fall of 1944 was a blur of small towns and villages. Somewhere in eastern France, I can't remember the exact name, we liberated a small town. The people were so grateful. An old woman gave me a bottle of wine she'd hidden from the Germans. Still have it, never opened it.\\n\\nInterviewer: Were you involved in the Battle of the Bulge?\\n\\n\",\n  \"file_size_bytes\": 5753,\n  \"content_length\": 2875\n}"},"isError":false}} { metadata: undefined }
