2025-12-20T17:18:14.998Z [iterm2] [info] Message from server: {"jsonrpc":"2.0","id":61,"result":{"content":[{"type":"text","text":"{\"success\":true,\"file_path\":\"/Users/happyhawking/Documents/Projects/Current/writer/README.md\",\"content\":\"# Oral History Book Research System\\n\\nA powerful CLI tool for managing WWII oral history transcripts, designed for book research. Veterans are first-class citizens - every search, every passage, every insight stays connected to the person who lived it.\\n\\n## Table of Contents\\n\\n- [Features](#features)\\n- [Installation](#installation)\\n- [Configuration](#configuration)\\n- [Quick Start](#quick-start)\\n- [Complete Command Reference](#complete-command-reference)\\n- [Workflows](#workflows)\\n- [Understanding the System](#understanding-the-system)\\n- [Tips & Best Practices](#tips--best-practices)\\n- [Troubleshooting](#troubleshooting)\\n\\n---\\n\\n## Features\\n\\n- **Veteran-centric organization**: Veterans are characters in your book, not just metadata\\n- **LLM-powered extraction**: Automatically extracts dates, people, places, and notable stories\\n- **Narrative Synthesis**: Stepped-back analysis of a veteran's entire story and narrative potential\\n- **Topic Synthesis**: Consolidated accounts of specific historical topics across multiple veterans\\n- **Semantic + keyword search**: Find relevant passages across all testimonies using AI embeddings\\n- **Timeline synthesis**: Chronological view of events across veterans\\n- **Relationship mapping**: Track people mentioned and cross-veteran connections\\n- **Writing potential scoring**: Identifies high-value passages for your book\\n- **Chapter assignment**: Organize material by book chapter\\n- **Markdown export**: Generate research documents ready for writing\\n- **Project statistics**: Comprehensive coverage analysis and metrics\\n- **Veteran comparison**: Side-by-side analysis of accounts on the same topic\\n- **Draft generation**: AI-powered first-draft narrative text with citations\\n\\n---\\n\\n## Installation\\n\\n### Prerequisites\\n\\n- Python 3.8 or higher\\n- API keys for LLM provider (Portkey, Anthropic, or OpenAI)\\n\\n### Setup Steps\\n\\n```bash\\n# Clone or copy the project\\ncd writer\\n\\n# Create virtual environment\\npython -m venv venv\\nsource venv/bin/activate  # On Windows: venv\\\\Scripts\\\\activate\\n\\n# Install dependencies\\npip install -r requirements.txt\\n\\n# Install the CLI tool\\npip install -e .\\n```\\n\\n### Verify Installation\\n\\n```bash\\noralhistory --version\\n# Should output: 1.0.0\\n```\\n\\n---\\n\\n## Configuration\\n\\n### Environment Variables\\n\\nCreate a `.env` file in the project root with your API keys:\\n\\n```bash\\n# For Portkey (recommended)\\nPORTKEY_API_KEY=your_portkey_key_here\\nPORTKEY_MODEL=claude-sonnet-4\\nPORTKEY_EMBEDDING_MODEL=text-embedding-3-small\\n\\n# Alternative: Direct provider keys\\nANTHROPIC_API_KEY=your_key_here\\nOPENAI_API_KEY=your_key_here\\n```\\n\\n### Project Configuration\\n\\nWhen you run `oralhistory init`, a `config.yaml` file is created. You can customize:\\n\\n- **LLM Provider**: Choose between `portkey`, `anthropic`, `openai`, or `ollama`\\n- **Search Settings**: Hybrid search weights, result limits\\n- **Embedding Model**: Which model to use for semantic search\\n\\n---\\n\\n## Quick Start\\n\\n```bash\\n# 1. Initialize a new project\\noralhistory init my_book_research\\ncd my_book_research\\n\\n# 2. Ingest your first transcript\\noralhistory ingest ../transcripts/mitchell_1995.txt --veteran \\\"James Mitchell\\\"\\n\\n# 3. View the veteran profile and researcher's brief\\noralhistory veteran mitchell_james\\noralhistory profile mitchell_james\\n\\n# 4. Search for specific topics or synthesize a narrative\\noralhistory search \\\"Battle of the Bulge\\\"\\noralhistory topic \\\"Battle of the Bulge Bastogne\\\"\\n\\n# 5. View a full response\\noralhistory view mitchell_james 005\\n\\n# 6. Assign material to chapters\\noralhistory annotate mitchell_james 005 --chapter 3 --note \\\"Great opening scene\\\"\\n\\n# 7. Export for writing\\noralhistory export chapter 3\\n\\n# 8. View project statistics\\noralhistory stats\\n\\n# 9. Compare two veterans on a topic\\noralhistory compare veteran1 veteran2 --topic \\\"Bastogne\\\" --analyze\\n\\n# 10. Generate first draft\\noralhistory draft --topic \\\"tank combat\\\" --style narrative\\n```\\n\\n---\\n\\n## Complete Command Reference\\n\\n### Project Management\\n\\n#### `oralhistory init [--path PATH]`\\n\\nInitialize a new oral history research project.\\n\\n**What it does:**\\n- Creates project directory structure\\n- Generates `config.yaml` with default settings\\n- Creates folders: `veterans/`, `annotations/`, `index/`, `exports/`\\n\\n**Example:**\\n```bash\\noralhistory init my_book_research\\n# Creates: my_book_research/\\n#          \\u251c\\u2500\\u2500 config.yaml\\n#          \\u251c\\u2500\\u2500 veterans/\\n#          \\u251c\\u2500\\u2500 annotations/\\n#          \\u251c\\u2500\\u2500 index/\\n#          \\u2514\\u2500\\u2500 exports/\\n```\\n\\n**Note:** You must be in a project directory (or have one initialized) to run most other commands.\\n\\n---\\n\\n#### `oralhistory veterans`\\n\\nList all veterans in your project.\\n\\n**Output:**\\n- Table showing: ID, Name, Unit, Number of Responses\\n- Quick overview of your research corpus\\n\\n**Example:**\\n```bash\\noralhistory veterans\\n\\n# Output:\\n# \\u250c\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u252c\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u252c\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u252c\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2510\\n# \\u2502 ID                  \\u2502 Name             \\u2502 Unit                     \\u2502 Responses \\u2502\\n# \\u251c\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u253c\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u253c\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u253c\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2524\\n# \\u2502 mitchell_james      \\u2502 James Mitchell   \\u2502 101st Airborne Division  \\u2502 45        \\u2502\\n# \\u2502 smith_john          \\u2502 John Smith       \\u2502 6th Armored Division     \\u2502 38        \\u2502\\n# \\u2514\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2534\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2534\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2534\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2518\\n```\\n\\n---\\n\\n#### `oralhistory stats [--detailed]`\\n\\nShow project-wide statistics and coverage analysis.\\n\\n**What it shows:**\\n- **Summary counts**: Total veterans, responses, paragraphs, verified status, annotated responses, HIGH potential passages\\n- **Topic coverage**: Bar chart of most common topics across all veterans\\n- **Time coverage**: Bar chart showing distribution by year (1942-1945)\\n- **Top locations**: Frequency list of most mentioned locations\\n- **Writing potential distribution**: Breakdown of HIGH/MEDIUM/LOW passages with percentages\\n\\n**Options:**\\n- `--detailed`: Show per-veteran breakdown table\\n\\n**Example:**\\n```bash\\n# Basic statistics\\noralhistory stats\\n\\n# Detailed view with per-veteran stats\\noralhistory stats --detailed\\n```\\n\\n**Output includes:**\\n```\\n\\u2554\\u2550\\u2550\\u2550\\u2550\\u2550\\u2550\\u2550\\u2550\\u2550\\u2550\\u2550\\u2550\\u2550\\u2550\\u2550\\u2550\\u2550\\u2550\\u2550\\u2550\\u2550\\u2550\\u2550\\u2550\\u2550\\u2550\\u2550\\u2550\\u2550\\u2550\\u2550\\u2550\\u2550\\u2550\\u2550\\u2550\\u2550\\u2550\\u2550\\u2550\\u2550\\u2550\\u2550\\u2550\\u2550\\u2550\\u2550\\u2550\\u2550\\u2550\\u2550\\u2550\\u2550\\u2550\\u2550\\u2550\\u2550\\u2550\\u2550\\u2550\\u2550\\u2550\\u2557\\n\\u2551 PROJECT STATISTICS                                           \\u2551\\n\\u255a\\u2550\\u2550\\u2550\\u2550\\u2550\\u2550\\u2550\\u2550\\u2550\\u2550\\u2550\\u2550\\u2550\\u2550\\u2550\\u2550\\u2550\\u2550\\u2550\\u2550\\u2550\\u2550\\u2550\\u2550\\u2550\\u2550\\u2550\\u2550\\u2550\\u2550\\u2550\\u2550\\u2550\\u2550\\u2550\\u2550\\u2550\\u2550\\u2550\\u2550\\u2550\\u2550\\u2550\\u2550\\u2550\\u2550\\u2550\\u2550\\u2550\\u2550\\u2550\\u2550\\u2550\\u2550\\u2550\\u2550\\u2550\\u2550\\u2550\\u2550\\u2550\\u2550\\u255d\\n\\n Veterans:     5       Responses: 487     Paragraphs: 1,243\\n Verified:     2/5     Annotated: 156     HIGH potential: 89\\n\\n TOPIC COVERAGE\\n \\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\n combat        \\u2588\\u2588\\u2588\\u2588\\u2588\\u2588\\u2588\\u2588\\u2588\\u2588\\u2588\\u2588\\u2588\\u2588\\u2588\\u2588\\u2588\\u2588\\u2588\\u2588\\u2588\\u2588\\u2588\\u2588  142\\n training      \\u2588\\u2588\\u2588\\u2588\\u2588\\u2588\\u2588\\u2588\\u2588\\u2588\\u2588\\u2588              67\\n equipment     \\u2588\\u2588\\u2588\\u2588\\u2588\\u2588\\u2588\\u2588                  45\\n ...\\n\\n TIME COVERAGE\\n \\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\n 1942 \\u2588\\u2588\\n 1943 \\u2588\\u2588\\u2588\\u2588\\n 1944 \\u2588\\u2588\\u2588\\u2588\\u2588\\u2588\\u2588\\u2588\\u2588\\u2588\\u2588\\u2588\\u2588\\u2588\\u2588\\u2588\\u2588\\u2588\\u2588\\u2588\\u2588\\u2588\\u2588\\u2588\\u2588\\u2588\\u2588\\u2588\\u2588\\u2588\\u2588\\u2588\\n 1945 \\u2588\\u2588\\u2588\\u2588\\u2588\\u2588\\u2588\\u2588\\u2588\\u2588\\u2588\\u2588\\n```\\n\\n**Use case:** Get a bird's-eye view of your research corpus, identify coverage gaps, and see which topics are most represented.\\n\\n---\\n\\n### Ingestion\\n\\n#### `oralhistory ingest <transcript_file> --veteran \\\"Name\\\" [OPTIONS]`\\n\\nParse and ingest a transcript file into the system.\\n\\n**Required:**\\n- `transcript_file`: Path to transcript text file\\n- `--veteran, -v`: Full name of the veteran\\n\\n**Options:**\\n- `--unit, -u`: Military unit (e.g., \\\"101st Airborne Division\\\")\\n- `--rank, -r`: Military rank (e.g., \\\"Sergeant\\\", \\\"Captain\\\")\\n- `--source, -s`: Source description or location\\n- `--dry-run`: Preview what would be extracted without saving\\n- `--no-embeddings`: Skip embedding generation (faster, but no semantic search)\\n\\n**What happens:**\\n1. **Parsing**: Splits transcript into Q&A responses (handles various formats including single-line transcripts with `>>`, `-`, and named speaker markers)\\n2. **LLM Extraction**: Extracts metadata (dates, people, places, topics)\\n3. **Synthesis**: Creates timeline, relationships, notable stories\\n4. **Indexing**: Generates embeddings for semantic search\\n5. **Saving**: Creates veteran YAML file and annotation file\\n\\n**Example:**\\n```bash\\n# Full ingestion with all metadata\\noralhistory ingest transcript.txt --veteran \\\"Hugo Victor Jarussi\\\" \\\\\\n  --unit \\\"603rd Tank Destroyer Battalion\\\" \\\\\\n  --rank \\\"Sergeant\\\"\\n\\n# Quick ingestion without embeddings (faster, for testing)\\noralhistory ingest transcript.txt --veteran \\\"John Smith\\\" --no-embeddings\\n\\n# Preview what will be extracted\\noralhistory ingest transcript.txt --veteran \\\"John Smith\\\" --dry-run\\n```\\n\\n**Understanding the Output:**\\n- `\\u2713 Parsed: N responses` - How many Q&A pairs were found\\n- `\\u2713 Extracted metadata` - LLM has analyzed each response\\n- `\\u2713 Timeline: N events` - Chronological events extracted\\n- `\\u2713 Relationships: N people` - People mentioned identified\\n- `\\u2713 Notable stories: N` - High-value narrative moments flagged\\n- `\\u26a0 Verification needed` - Items that may need human review\\n\\n**Important:** If a veteran already exists, you'll be prompted to confirm overwrite. Re-ingesting will:\\n- \\u2705 Replace all responses with new parsed data\\n- \\u2705 Regenerate all embeddings\\n- \\u2705 Regenerate LLM-extracted metadata\\n- \\u2705 **Preserve** your annotations (notes, chapters, flags)\\n\\n---\\n\\n### Synthesis & Analysis\\n\\n#### `oralhistory profile <veteran_id> [--refresh]`\\n\\nGenerate and view a high-level \\\"Researcher's Brief\\\" for a single veteran.\\n\\n**What it shows:**\\n- **Overview**: A high-level summary of the veteran's story.\\n- **Narrative Style**: Analysis of how they tell their story (e.g., technical, emotional, sensory).\\n- **Thematic Strengths**: Specific areas where this veteran provides high-quality info (e.g., \\\"Combat\\\", \\\"Logistics\\\").\\n- **Writing Advice**: Specific tips on how to use this testimony in your book.\\n- **Key Entities**: Frequenty mentioned units, places, or terms.\\n\\n**Example:**\\n```bash\\noralhistory profile mitchell_james\\n```\\n\\n**Note:** The profile is automatically generated during ingestion, but you can use `--refresh` to regenerate it if you've edited the transcript or want a fresh take.\\n\\n---\\n\\n#### `oralhistory topic \\\"<query>\\\" [--limit N]`\\n\\nSynthesize a cohesive narrative on a specific topic across **all** veterans in your project.\\n\\n**What it does:**\\n1. Searches the entire project for the most relevant passages.\\n2. Uses an LLM to write a 3-5 paragraph \\\"Consolidated Account\\\".\\n3. Automatically inserts **Source Citations** (e.g., `[mitchell_james:012]`).\\n4. Highlights unique details and potential contradictions.\\n\\n**Example:**\\n```bash\\noralhistory topic \\\"C-rations and hunger\\\"\\noralhistory topic \\\"Bastogne winter conditions\\\"\\n```\\n\\n**Use case:** Perfect for the initial draft of a scene or chapter section where you need to combine multiple perspectives into one narrative.\\n\\n---\\n\\n#### `oralhistory compare <veteran1_id> <veteran2_id> --topic \\\"<query>\\\" [OPTIONS]`\\n\\nSide-by-side comparison of two veterans' accounts on the same topic.\\n\\n**What it does:**\\n1. Searches both veterans' testimonies for the specified topic\\n2. Displays matching passages in side-by-side panels\\n3. Optionally generates LLM analysis comparing their perspectives\\n\\n**Options:**\\n- `--topic, -t`: Topic to compare on (required)\\n- `--limit, -l N`: Maximum passages per veteran (default: 5)\\n- `--analyze`: Use LLM to generate comparison analysis\\n\\n**Example:**\\n```bash\\n# Basic comparison\\noralhistory compare rolfes_john_w jarussi_hugo_victor --topic \\\"Bastogne\\\"\\n\\n# With LLM analysis\\noralhistory compare rolfes_john_w pearson_john --topic \\\"training\\\" --analyze\\n\\n# Limit results\\noralhistory compare veteran1 veteran2 --topic \\\"combat\\\" --limit 3\\n```\\n\\n**Output:**\\n- Side-by-side panels showing each veteran's matching passages\\n- Response IDs and writing potential indicators\\n- LLM analysis (if `--analyze` used) highlighting:\\n  - Areas of agreement or overlapping details\\n  - Unique perspectives each veteran provides\\n  - Contradictions or different viewpoints\\n  - Which veteran provides more detail on specific aspects\\n\\n**Use case:** Compare how different veterans experienced the same event or topic. Great for identifying complementary perspectives or contradictions that need resolution.\\n\\n---\\n\\n#### `oralhistory draft [OPTIONS]`\\n\\nGenerate first-draft narrative text from selected passages with inline citations.\\n\\n**What it does:**\\n1. Collects relevant passages based on your filters\\n2. Uses LLM to weave the actual passages into narrative prose\\n3. Converts spoken language to written language while preserving authentic voice\\n4. Quotes extensively from the original accounts with inline citations like `(Rolfes, Response 014)`\\n5. Uses `(VeteranName, Response XXX, adapted)` for passages slightly edited for written flow\\n6. Displays sources used and word count\\n\\n**Options:**\\n- `--topic, -t TOPIC`: Filter by topic\\n- `--veteran, -v ID`: Filter to specific veteran\\n- `--chapter, -c N`: Filter to responses in chapter N\\n- `--query, -q QUERY`: Search query to find passages\\n- `--limit, -l N`: Maximum passages to use (default: 10)\\n- `--style, -s STYLE`: Writing style - `narrative`, `documentary`, or `academic` (default: `narrative`)\\n- `--export PATH`: Export draft to markdown file\\n\\n**Note:** You must specify at least one filter (`--topic`, `--veteran`, `--chapter`, or `--query`).\\n\\n**Examples:**\\n```bash\\n# Generate narrative draft on a topic\\noralhistory draft --topic \\\"tank combat\\\" --style narrative\\n\\n# Draft from specific veteran's chapter\\noralhistory draft --veteran rolfes_john_w --chapter 3 --style documentary\\n\\n# Draft from search results\\noralhistory draft --query \\\"Christmas 1944\\\" --limit 5 --style academic\\n\\n# Export to file\\noralhistory draft --topic \\\"training\\\" --export training_draft.md\\n```\\n\\n**Writing Styles:**\\n- **narrative**: Compelling, human narrative style with vivid scenes and sensory details\\n- **documentary**: Objective, journalistic style with extensive quotes\\n- **academic**: Scholarly style with analytical framework and formal citations\\n\\n**Output includes:**\\n- Generated draft text (500-800 words)\\n- List of all sources used with response IDs\\n- Word count\\n- Option to export to markdown file\\n\\n**Use case:** Generate first-draft prose ready for editing. Perfect for turning research into narrative text with proper citations. The LLM uses the veterans' actual words extensively, converting spoken language to written language while preserving their authentic voice. The draft tells the story through the passages themselves, not just summaries.\\n\\n**Note:** The draft tool prioritizes using the original passages. It will quote liberally from the accounts and make minimal edits to convert spoken language (removing filler words, fixing grammar) to written language while preserving the veteran's voice and specific details.\\n\\n---\\n\\n### Search & Exploration\\n\\n#### `oralhistory search <query> [OPTIONS]`\\n\\nSearch across all veterans using semantic + keyword hybrid search.\\n\\n**Options:**\\n- `--limit, -l N`: Maximum results (default: 20)\\n- `--veteran, -v ID`: Filter to specific veteran\\n- `--chapter, -c N`: Filter to responses in chapter N\\n- `--topic, -t TOPIC`: Filter by extracted topic\\n- `--potential, -p LEVEL`: Filter by writing potential (`low`, `medium`, `high`)\\n- `--export FILENAME`: Export results to markdown file\\n\\n**How it works:**\\n- Uses AI embeddings for semantic understanding\\n- Combines with keyword matching for precision\\n- Ranks by relevance score\\n- Shows truncated excerpts (use `view` for full text)\\n\\n**Examples:**\\n```bash\\n# Basic search\\noralhistory search \\\"tank destroyer\\\"\\n\\n# Find high-potential passages about a specific topic\\noralhistory search \\\"food rations hardship\\\" --potential high\\n\\n# Search within one veteran's testimony\\noralhistory search \\\"training\\\" --veteran mitchell_james\\n\\n# Find material already assigned to a chapter\\noralhistory search \\\"combat\\\" --chapter 5\\n\\n# Export search results for later review\\noralhistory search \\\"Battle of the Bulge\\\" --export bulge_research.md\\n```\\n\\n**Understanding Results:**\\n- Results grouped by veteran\\n- Each result shows: Response ID, Chapter (if assigned), Writing Potential\\n- Truncated question and answer (use `view` for full text)\\n- Your notes and flags are displayed\\n- LLM summary and writing notes included\\n\\n---\\n\\n#### `oralhistory veteran <veteran_id> [OPTIONS]`\\n\\nShow detailed profile of a single veteran.\\n\\n**Options:**\\n- `--timeline`: Show only timeline\\n- `--relationships`: Show only relationships\\n- `--responses`: Show all responses (list view)\\n- `--response ID`: Show specific response in detail\\n\\n**Default view shows:**\\n- Basic info (name, unit, rank, service dates)\\n- Timeline (chronological events)\\n- Relationships (people mentioned)\\n- Notable stories (high-value passages)\\n- Verification items (things needing review)\\n\\n**Examples:**\\n```bash\\n# Full profile\\noralhistory veteran mitchell_james\\n\\n# Just the timeline\\noralhistory veteran mitchell_james --timeline\\n\\n# Just relationships\\noralhistory veteran mitchell_james --relationships\\n\\n# List all responses\\noralhistory veteran mitchell_james --responses\\n\\n# View specific response\\noralhistory veteran mitchell_james --response 005\\n```\\n\\n**Understanding Verification Items:**\\n- These are flags the LLM creates when it detects potential issues\\n- Common types: Name discrepancies, timeline precision, unit details, geographic precision\\n- Each item may have `response_ids` showing which responses are related\\n- **What to do:**\\n  - If metadata is correct \\u2192 Remove the item from YAML or ignore\\n  - If metadata is wrong \\u2192 Fix it, then remove the item\\n  - If uncertain \\u2192 Add annotation note and investigate\\n  - **New:** Use `oralhistory verify <veteran_id>` for an interactive wizard to resolve these items.\\n\\n---\\n\\n#### `oralhistory verify <veteran_id>`\\n\\nInteractive wizard to audit and correct a veteran's data.\\n\\n**What it does:**\\n1. **Identity Check**: Confirm or edit Name, Rank, and Unit.\\n2. **Timeline Audit**: Review events with low confidence or notes.\\n3. **Flag Resolution**: Walk through every \\\"Verification Needed\\\" item.\\n4. **Auto-Reindex**: Automatically updates the search index if you make changes.\\n\\n**Example:**\\n```bash\\noralhistory verify mitchell_james\\n```\\n\\n**Workflow:**\\n- Run this after `oralhistory ingest` to ensure your data is 100% accurate.\\n- Use `[v]iew sources` during the wizard to see the original transcript passages related to a flag.\\n\\n---\\n\\n#### `oralhistory view <veteran_id> <response_id>`\\n\\nView the **complete, untruncated** text of a specific response.\\n\\n**What it shows:**\\n- Full interviewer question (no truncation)\\n- Full veteran answer (no truncation)\\n- Extracted metadata (summary, topics, locations, people, dates)\\n- Writing potential and LLM notes\\n- Your annotations (notes, flags, chapter assignment)\\n\\n**Use cases:**\\n- After finding interesting results in search\\n- When reviewing verification items\\n- Before assigning to chapters\\n- When writing and need full context\\n\\n**Example:**\\n```bash\\n# View response 005 from mitchell_james\\noralhistory view mitchell_james 005\\n\\n# After a search, view the full passage\\noralhistory search \\\"tank destroyer\\\"\\n# ... see interesting result [012] ...\\noralhistory view mitchell_james 012\\n```\\n\\n**Quick Actions Shown:**\\nThe view command displays hints for common follow-up actions:\\n- Assign to chapter\\n- Add note\\n- Export veteran\\n\\n---\\n\\n### Annotations\\n\\n#### `oralhistory annotate <veteran_id> [response_id] [OPTIONS]`\\n\\nAdd annotations to responses or veterans.\\n\\n**Response-level annotations:**\\n- `--note, -n \\\"text\\\"`: Add a note to a specific response\\n- `--chapter, -c N`: Assign response to chapter N\\n- `--flag FLAG`: Add a custom flag (e.g., \\\"needs-verification\\\", \\\"use-for-opening\\\")\\n- `--remove-flag FLAG`: Remove a flag\\n\\n**Veteran-level annotations:**\\n- `--veteran-note \\\"text\\\"`: Add note about the veteran (not specific to a response)\\n- `--todo \\\"text\\\"`: Add research todo item\\n\\n**Examples:**\\n```bash\\n# Add a note to a response\\noralhistory annotate mitchell_james 005 --note \\\"Great opening scene - use this\\\"\\n\\n# Assign to chapter\\noralhistory annotate mitchell_james 005 --chapter 3\\n\\n# Add multiple annotations at once\\noralhistory annotate mitchell_james 005 \\\\\\n  --chapter 3 \\\\\\n  --note \\\"Opening scene\\\" \\\\\\n  --flag \\\"high-priority\\\"\\n\\n# Add veteran-level note\\noralhistory annotate mitchell_james --veteran-note \\\"Introduce in chapter 2\\\"\\n\\n# Add research todo\\noralhistory annotate mitchell_james --todo \\\"Verify unit movements against history\\\"\\n\\n# Remove a flag\\noralhistory annotate mitchell_james 005 --remove-flag \\\"needs-verification\\\"\\n```\\n\\n**Best Practices:**\\n- Use flags for workflow states: `needs-verification`, `fact-checked`, `used-in-draft`\\n- Use notes for specific writing guidance\\n- Use chapters to organize material as you research\\n- Veteran notes are great for character development ideas\\n\\n---\\n\\n#### `oralhistory annotations [veteran_id] [OPTIONS]`\\n\\nView annotations.\\n\\n**Options:**\\n- `veteran_id`: Show annotations for specific veteran\\n- `--chapter, -c N`: Show all responses assigned to chapter N\\n- `--flags`: Show all flagged responses across all veterans\\n\\n**Examples:**\\n```bash\\n# View all annotations for a veteran\\noralhistory annotations mitchell_james\\n\\n# See what's assigned to chapter 5\\noralhistory annotations --chapter 5\\n\\n# Find all flagged responses\\noralhistory annotations --flags\\n```\\n\\n---\\n\\n### Chapter Management\\n\\n#### `oralhistory chapter <number> [--export]`\\n\\nView or export material assigned to a chapter.\\n\\n**What it shows:**\\n- All responses assigned to the chapter\\n- Grouped by veteran\\n- Your notes for each response\\n- Writing potential indicators\\n\\n**Options:**\\n- `--export`: Export chapter to markdown file\\n\\n**Example:**\\n```bash\\n# View chapter 3\\noralhistory chapter 3\\n\\n# Export chapter 3 for writing\\noralhistory chapter 3 --export\\n# Creates: exports/chapter_03.md\\n```\\n\\n**Use case:** This is your primary tool for organizing material as you research. Assign responses to chapters as you discover them, then export when ready to write.\\n\\n---\\n\\n### Export\\n\\n#### `oralhistory export chapter <number> [--filename FILE]`\\n\\nExport a chapter to markdown.\\n\\n**What's included:**\\n- All responses assigned to the chapter\\n- Full text (not truncated)\\n- Your annotations and notes\\n- Extracted metadata\\n- Organized by veteran\\n\\n**Example:**\\n```bash\\noralhistory export chapter 3\\n# Creates: exports/chapter_03.md\\n\\noralhistory export chapter 3 --filename my_chapter.md\\n# Creates: exports/my_chapter.md\\n```\\n\\n---\\n\\n#### `oralhistory export veteran <veteran_id> [--filename FILE]`\\n\\nExport all material for a veteran.\\n\\n**What's included:**\\n- Full profile (timeline, relationships, notable stories)\\n- All responses with full text\\n- Your annotations\\n- Verification items\\n- Ready for character development or fact-checking\\n\\n**Example:**\\n```bash\\noralhistory export veteran mitchell_james\\n# Creates: exports/mitchell_james.md\\n```\\n\\n---\\n\\n#### `oralhistory export stories [--filename FILE]`\\n\\nExport all notable stories across all veterans.\\n\\n**What's included:**\\n- All stories flagged as high writing potential\\n- Organized by veteran\\n- Response references\\n- LLM writing notes\\n\\n**Use case:** Quick way to find the best narrative moments across your entire corpus.\\n\\n**Example:**\\n```bash\\noralhistory export stories\\n# Creates: exports/notable_stories.md\\n```\\n\\n---\\n\\n#### `oralhistory export timeline [--start YYYY-MM] [--end YYYY-MM] [--filename FILE]`\\n\\nExport combined timeline across all veterans.\\n\\n**Options:**\\n- `--start`: Filter to events after this date\\n- `--end`: Filter to events before this date\\n\\n**What's included:**\\n- Chronological list of all events\\n- Grouped by date\\n- Shows which veteran(s) mentioned each event\\n- Response references for verification\\n\\n**Example:**\\n```bash\\n# Full timeline\\noralhistory export timeline\\n\\n# Timeline for specific period\\noralhistory export timeline --start 1944-06 --end 1944-12\\n# Creates: exports/timeline_1944-06_to_1944-12.md\\n```\\n\\n---\\n\\n### Maintenance\\n\\n#### `oralhistory reindex [--no-embeddings]`\\n\\nRebuild search indexes for all veterans.\\n\\n**When to use:**\\n- After manually editing veteran YAML files\\n- If search seems broken or incomplete\\n- After updating embeddings model\\n- If you want to regenerate embeddings without re-ingesting\\n\\n**Options:**\\n- `--no-embeddings`: Skip embedding generation (faster, but semantic search won't work)\\n\\n**Example:**\\n```bash\\n# Full reindex with embeddings\\noralhistory reindex\\n\\n# Reindex structure only (no API calls)\\noralhistory reindex --no-embeddings\\n```\\n\\n**Note:** This preserves your annotations. Only the search index is rebuilt.\\n\\n---\\n\\n## Workflows\\n\\n### Workflow 1: Initial Research Phase\\n\\n**Goal:** Ingest multiple transcripts and get overview\\n\\n```bash\\n# 1. Initialize project\\noralhistory init my_book\\ncd my_book\\n\\n# 2. Ingest multiple transcripts\\noralhistory ingest transcript1.txt --veteran \\\"James Mitchell\\\"\\noralhistory ingest transcript2.txt --veteran \\\"John Smith\\\"\\noralhistory ingest transcript3.txt --veteran \\\"Robert Jones\\\"\\n\\n# 3. Get overview\\noralhistory veterans  # See who you have\\noralhistory veteran mitchell_james  # Review each profile\\n\\n# 4. Find notable stories\\noralhistory export stories  # See best narrative moments\\n\\n# 5. Search for themes\\noralhistory search \\\"combat experience\\\" --potential high\\noralhistory search \\\"daily life\\\" --potential high\\n```\\n\\n---\\n\\n### Workflow 2: Organizing Material by Chapter\\n\\n**Goal:** Assign material to chapters as you research\\n\\n```bash\\n# 1. Search for material relevant to chapter 3\\noralhistory search \\\"training camp\\\" --potential high\\n\\n# 2. Review full passages\\noralhistory view mitchell_james 012\\noralhistory view smith_john 005\\n\\n# 3. Assign to chapter with notes\\noralhistory annotate mitchell_james 012 --chapter 3 --note \\\"Opening scene\\\"\\noralhistory annotate smith_john 005 --chapter 3 --note \\\"Supporting detail\\\"\\n\\n# 4. Review chapter organization\\noralhistory chapter 3\\n\\n# 5. Export when ready to write\\noralhistory export chapter 3\\n```\\n\\n---\\n\\n### Workflow 3: Character Development\\n\\n**Goal:** Deep dive into a single veteran's story\\n\\n```bash\\n# 1. View full profile\\noralhistory veteran mitchell_james\\n\\n# 2. Review timeline\\noralhistory veteran mitchell_james --timeline\\n\\n# 3. See relationships\\noralhistory veteran mitchell_james --relationships\\n\\n# 4. Read notable stories\\n# (shown in profile, or export stories)\\n\\n# 5. Export for character work\\noralhistory export veteran mitchell_james\\n\\n# 6. Add character notes\\noralhistory annotate mitchell_james --veteran-note \\\"Strong voice, use for POV character\\\"\\n```\\n\\n---\\n\\n### Workflow 4: Fact-Checking & Verification\\n\\n**Goal:** Verify information and resolve discrepancies\\n\\n```bash\\n# 1. Check verification items\\noralhistory veteran mitchell_james\\n# Look for \\\"VERIFICATION NEEDED\\\" section\\n\\n# 2. View related responses\\noralhistory view mitchell_james 001  # If response_ids shown\\n\\n# 3. Add verification notes\\noralhistory annotate mitchell_james 001 --note \\\"Verified: Name correct as James Mitchell\\\"\\noralhistory annotate mitchell_james 001 --flag \\\"fact-checked\\\"\\n\\n# 4. Find all unverified items\\noralhistory annotations --flags | grep -v fact-checked\\n\\n# 5. Export for external verification\\noralhistory export veteran mitchell_james\\n```\\n\\n---\\n\\n### Workflow 5: Writing Phase\\n\\n**Goal:** Generate material ready for writing\\n\\n```bash\\n# 1. Review chapter organization\\noralhistory chapter 5\\n\\n# 2. Export chapter\\noralhistory export chapter 5\\n# Opens exports/chapter_05.md in your editor\\n\\n# 3. As you write, flag used material\\noralhistory annotate mitchell_james 012 --flag \\\"used-in-draft\\\"\\n\\n# 4. Find additional material if needed\\noralhistory search \\\"specific detail\\\" --chapter 5\\n\\n# 5. Export timeline for chronological reference\\noralhistory export timeline --start 1944-06 --end 1944-12\\n```\\n\\n---\\n\\n### Workflow 6: Narrative Development\\n\\n**Goal:** Step back from details to build your book's narrative arc\\n\\n```bash\\n# 1. Get a researcher's brief for your main character\\noralhistory profile jarussi_hugo_victor\\n\\n# 2. Synthesize a specific historical event\\noralhistory topic \\\"crossing the Rhine pontoon bridge\\\"\\n\\n# 3. Compare perspectives on a specific hardship\\noralhistory topic \\\"winter 1944 cold and equipment\\\"\\n\\n# 4. Use synthesized accounts as a first draft or outline\\n# (Topic synthesis provides grounded citations for easy verification)\\n```\\n\\n---\\n\\n### Workflow 7: Project Overview & Statistics\\n\\n**Goal:** Get a comprehensive view of your research corpus\\n\\n```bash\\n# 1. View project statistics\\noralhistory stats\\n\\n# 2. See detailed breakdown\\noralhistory stats --detailed\\n\\n# 3. Compare two veterans on a topic\\noralhistory compare rolfes_john_w jarussi_hugo_victor --topic \\\"Bastogne\\\" --analyze\\n\\n# 4. Generate first draft from research\\noralhistory draft --topic \\\"tank combat\\\" --style narrative --export draft.md\\n```\\n\\n---\\n\\n### Workflow 8: Draft Generation\\n\\n**Goal:** Turn research into first-draft narrative prose\\n\\n```bash\\n# 1. Generate draft from topic search\\noralhistory draft --topic \\\"Christmas 1944\\\" --style narrative\\n\\n# 2. Generate draft from chapter material\\noralhistory draft --chapter 5 --style documentary\\n\\n# 3. Generate draft from specific veteran\\noralhistory draft --veteran rolfes_john_w --limit 10 --style narrative\\n\\n# 4. Export for editing\\noralhistory draft --query \\\"training camp\\\" --export training_draft.md\\n```\\n\\n---\\n\\n## Understanding the System\\n\\n### Data Model\\n\\n**Veteran** (Primary Entity)\\n- The central organizing principle\\n- Contains: Basic info, timeline, relationships, notable stories, responses\\n- Stored in: `veterans/<veteran_id>.yaml`\\n\\n**Response** (Atomic Unit)\\n- One interviewer question + veteran's complete answer\\n- Contains: Full text, extracted metadata, writing potential score\\n- Identified by: `<veteran_id>:<response_id>` (e.g., `mitchell_james:005`)\\n\\n**Paragraph** (Search Precision)\\n- Subdivisions of responses for precise search\\n- Used internally for embedding generation\\n\\n**Narrative Synthesis** (High-Level View)\\n- **Veteran Profile**: A \\\"briefing\\\" on one veteran's strengths and style.\\n- **Topic Synthesis**: A \\\"consolidated account\\\" of a topic across multiple veterans.\\n- These layers help prevent \\\"detail loss\\\" by providing a wide-angle lens on your research.\\n\\n**Annotations** (Your Layer)\\n- Your notes, chapter assignments, flags, todos\\n- Stored separately in: `annotations/<veteran_id>.yaml`\\n- **Preserved** when re-ingesting transcripts\\n\\n### Writing Potential Scores\\n\\nThe LLM scores each response:\\n\\n- **HIGH** \\u2b50: Vivid sensory detail, action sequences, named characters, emotional moments, unique perspectives\\n- **MEDIUM**: Useful context, fills gaps, corroborates other accounts\\n- **LOW**: Routine biographical info, vague recollections, administrative details\\n\\n**Use `--potential high` in search** to find the best material quickly.\\n\\n### Search Technology\\n\\n- **Semantic Search**: Uses AI embeddings to understand meaning, not just keywords\\n- **Keyword Search**: Traditional text matching for precision\\n- **Hybrid**: Combines both (default: 70% semantic, 30% keyword)\\n- **Embeddings**: Generated using Portkey/OpenAI embeddings API\\n- **Storage**: SQLite database with vector similarity search\\n\\n### File Structure\\n\\n```\\nmy_book_research/\\n\\u251c\\u2500\\u2500 config.yaml              # Project configuration\\n\\u251c\\u2500\\u2500 veterans/                # Veteran data (auto-generated)\\n\\u2502   \\u2514\\u2500\\u2500 mitchell_james.yaml\\n\\u251c\\u2500\\u2500 annotations/             # Your notes (preserved on re-ingest)\\n\\u2502   \\u2514\\u2500\\u2500 mitchell_james.yaml\\n\\u251c\\u2500\\u2500 index/                   # Search database\\n\\u2502   \\u2514\\u2500\\u2500 embeddings.sqlite\\n\\u2514\\u2500\\u2500 exports/                 # Generated markdown files\\n    \\u251c\\u2500\\u2500 chapter_03.md\\n    \\u2514\\u2500\\u2500 mitchell_james.md\\n```\\n\\n**Important:** \\n- `veterans/` files are regenerated on re-ingest\\n- `annotations/` files are **preserved** on re-ingest\\n- `index/` is rebuilt with `reindex` command\\n- `exports/` can be deleted and regenerated anytime\\n\\n---\\n\\n## Tips & Best Practices\\n\\n### 1. Start Broad, Then Narrow\\n\\n- Ingest multiple transcripts before diving deep\\n- Use `oralhistory veterans` to get overview\\n- Use `oralhistory export stories` to find best moments\\n- Then focus on specific veterans or chapters\\n\\n### 2. Use Writing Potential Scores\\n\\n- Filter searches with `--potential high` to find best material\\n- Notable stories are pre-flagged as high potential\\n- Don't ignore medium/low - they provide context\\n\\n### 3. Organize as You Research\\n\\n- Assign responses to chapters as you discover them\\n- Add notes immediately while context is fresh\\n- Use flags for workflow: `needs-verification`, `fact-checked`, `used-in-draft`\\n\\n### 4. Leverage Search Power\\n\\n- Use natural language queries: \\\"food shortages during winter\\\"\\n- Combine filters: `--potential high --chapter 5`\\n- Export search results for later review\\n\\n### 5. Export Early, Export Often\\n\\n- Generate chapter exports as you assign material\\n- Export veterans for character development work\\n- Export timeline for chronological reference\\n- Exports are markdown - edit them directly if needed\\n\\n### 6. Handle Verification Items\\n\\n- Review verification items after ingestion\\n- If metadata is correct \\u2192 Remove from YAML or ignore\\n- If metadata is wrong \\u2192 Fix it, then remove item\\n- Add annotation notes to document your verification\\n\\n### 7. Use Git for Version Control\\n\\n```bash\\ngit init\\ngit add .\\ngit commit -m \\\"Initial ingestion: 3 veterans\\\"\\n# Commit after each research session\\n```\\n\\n### 8. Re-ingesting Transcripts\\n\\n**What happens:**\\n- \\u2705 Responses replaced with new parsed data\\n- \\u2705 Embeddings regenerated\\n- \\u2705 LLM metadata regenerated\\n- \\u2705 **Your annotations preserved**\\n\\n**Best practice:**\\n- Use `--dry-run` first to preview\\n- Export important data before re-ingesting\\n- Consider manual edits vs. re-ingest trade-offs\\n\\n### 9. Performance Tips\\n\\n- Use `--no-embeddings` for quick testing\\n- Reindex only when needed\\n- Search is fast once indexed\\n- Large projects (100+ veterans) work fine\\n\\n### 10. Troubleshooting Workflow\\n\\n1. **Search not finding things?** \\u2192 Run `oralhistory reindex`\\n2. **Wrong data?** \\u2192 Check verification items, edit YAML manually\\n3. **Missing responses?** \\u2192 Check transcript format, parser may need adjustment\\n4. **API errors?** \\u2192 Check `.env` file, verify API keys\\n\\n---\\n\\n## Troubleshooting\\n\\n### \\\"Not in an oralhistory project\\\"\\n\\n**Problem:** Command fails with this error.\\n\\n**Solution:**\\n```bash\\n# Make sure you're in a project directory\\ncd my_book_research\\n\\n# Or initialize a new project\\noralhistory init .\\n```\\n\\n---\\n\\n### \\\"Veteran not found\\\"\\n\\n**Problem:** Can't find veteran by ID.\\n\\n**Solution:**\\n```bash\\n# List all veterans to see correct IDs\\noralhistory veterans\\n\\n# IDs are generated from names: \\\"James Mitchell\\\" \\u2192 \\\"mitchell_james\\\"\\n# Use the exact ID shown in the list\\n```\\n\\n---\\n\\n### Search Returns No Results\\n\\n**Problem:** Search finds nothing even when you know material exists.\\n\\n**Solutions:**\\n1. **Check if embeddings were generated:**\\n   ```bash\\n   # Reindex to regenerate embeddings\\n   oralhistory reindex\\n   ```\\n\\n2. **Try different search terms:**\\n   ```bash\\n   # More specific\\n   oralhistory search \\\"tank destroyer battalion\\\"\\n   \\n   # More general\\n   oralhistory search \\\"tank\\\"\\n   ```\\n\\n3. **Check filters:**\\n   ```bash\\n   # Remove filters that might be too restrictive\\n   oralhistory search \\\"combat\\\"  # Without --chapter or --potential\\n   ```\\n\\n---\\n\\n### Verification Items with Empty Details\\n\\n**Problem:** Verification items show but have no detail text.\\n\\n**Explanation:** The LLM flagged an issue but didn't provide specifics. This often happens with general transcript-level concerns.\\n\\n**What to do:**\\n- If metadata looks correct \\u2192 Ignore or remove from YAML\\n- If uncertain \\u2192 Review the transcript manually\\n- Check `response_ids` to see which responses are related\\n\\n---\\n\\n### Transcript Parses as Single Response\\n\\n**Problem:** Ingest shows \\\"Parsed: 1 responses\\\" when there should be many.\\n\\n**Causes:**\\n- Transcript is all on one line (no line breaks)\\n- Missing Q:/A: markers or speaker names\\n- Parser can't detect speaker patterns\\n\\n**Solutions:**\\n1. **The parser now handles single-line transcripts automatically** and recognizes:\\n   - `>>` chevron markers (converts to alternating Q:/A:)\\n   - `- Question? -` wrapped questions (converts to Q: format)\\n   - Named speakers like `HURST:`, `HALL:`, `Heldreth:` (adds newlines)\\n2. **Format transcript with Q:/A: markers:**\\n   ```\\n   Q: Question text here?\\n   \\n   A: Answer text here.\\n   \\n   Q: Next question?\\n   \\n   A: Next answer.\\n   ```\\n3. **Check transcript format** - parser looks for:\\n   - `Q:` / `A:` markers\\n   - `Interviewer:` / `Veteran:` markers\\n   - Speaker names followed by colons\\n   - `>>` chevron markers\\n   - `- Question? -` wrapped questions\\n\\n---\\n\\n### API Key Errors\\n\\n**Problem:** \\\"API key not set\\\" or authentication errors.\\n\\n**Solutions:**\\n1. **Check `.env` file exists and has correct keys:**\\n   ```bash\\n   cat .env\\n   # Should show: PORTKEY_API_KEY=... or ANTHROPIC_API_KEY=...\\n   ```\\n\\n2. **Verify keys are valid:**\\n   - Test with provider's API directly\\n   - Check for typos or extra spaces\\n\\n3. **Use correct environment variable names:**\\n   - Portkey: `PORTKEY_API_KEY`, `PORTKEY_MODEL`, `PORTKEY_EMBEDDING_MODEL`\\n   - Anthropic: `ANTHROPIC_API_KEY`\\n   - OpenAI: `OPENAI_API_KEY`\\n\\n---\\n\\n### Embedding Generation Fails\\n\\n**Problem:** Embeddings fail during ingest or reindex.\\n\\n**Solutions:**\\n1. **Skip embeddings temporarily:**\\n   ```bash\\n   oralhistory ingest transcript.txt --veteran \\\"Name\\\" --no-embeddings\\n   # Then reindex later when API is working\\n   ```\\n\\n2. **Check API quota/limits:**\\n   - Verify you have API credits\\n   - Check rate limits\\n\\n3. **Regenerate later:**\\n   ```bash\\n   oralhistory reindex  # Regenerates all embeddings\\n   ```\\n\\n---\\n\\n### Re-ingest Lost My Annotations\\n\\n**Problem:** After re-ingesting, annotations are gone.\\n\\n**This shouldn't happen!** Annotations are stored separately and preserved.\\n\\n**If it happened:**\\n1. Check `annotations/` directory - files should still exist\\n2. Annotations are in separate YAML files, not in veteran files\\n3. If truly lost, check git history or backups\\n\\n**Prevention:**\\n- Commit annotations to git before re-ingesting\\n- Export important annotations before major changes\\n\\n---\\n\\n### Chapter Export Missing Responses\\n\\n**Problem:** Chapter export doesn't include all assigned responses.\\n\\n**Solutions:**\\n1. **Verify assignments:**\\n   ```bash\\n   oralhistory annotations --chapter 5\\n   # Should show all responses assigned to chapter 5\\n   ```\\n\\n2. **Check response IDs match:**\\n   - Response IDs are like `001`, `002`, etc.\\n   - Must match exactly (case-sensitive)\\n\\n3. **Re-export:**\\n   ```bash\\n   oralhistory export chapter 5\\n   ```\\n\\n---\\n\\n## Advanced Usage\\n\\n### Manual YAML Editing\\n\\nYou can edit veteran YAML files directly:\\n\\n```bash\\n# Edit veteran file\\nvim veterans/mitchell_james.yaml\\n\\n# After editing, reindex to update search\\noralhistory reindex\\n```\\n\\n**What you can safely edit:**\\n- Basic info (name, unit, rank) - will be overwritten on re-ingest\\n- Timeline events - can add/remove\\n- Relationships - can modify\\n- Verification items - can remove resolved items\\n\\n**What to avoid:**\\n- Response IDs - must match annotations\\n- Response structure - breaking this breaks search\\n\\n### Custom Flags Workflow\\n\\nUse flags for your own workflow:\\n\\n```bash\\n# Mark for fact-checking\\noralhistory annotate mitchell_james 005 --flag \\\"needs-fact-check\\\"\\n\\n# Mark as verified\\noralhistory annotate mitchell_james 005 --flag \\\"fact-checked\\\"\\n\\n# Mark as used in draft\\noralhistory annotate mitchell_james 005 --flag \\\"used-in-draft\\\"\\n\\n# Find all unverified\\noralhistory annotations --flags | grep needs-fact-check\\n```\\n\\n### Batch Operations\\n\\nUse shell scripts for batch operations:\\n\\n```bash\\n# Ingest multiple transcripts\\nfor file in transcripts/*.txt; do\\n  name=$(basename \\\"$file\\\" .txt)\\n  oralhistory ingest \\\"$file\\\" --veteran \\\"$name\\\"\\ndone\\n\\n# Export all chapters\\nfor i in {1..10}; do\\n  oralhistory export chapter $i\\ndone\\n```\\n\\n---\\n\\n## License\\n\\nMIT License\\n\\n---\\n\\n## Getting Help\\n\\n- Check this README first\\n- Review command help: `oralhistory <command> --help`\\n- Check `specs.md` for technical details\\n- Review example outputs in terminal\\n\\n---\\n\\n**Happy researching! \\ud83c\\udf96\\ufe0f**\\n\",\"file_size_bytes\":40721,\"content_length\":39350}"}],"structuredContent":{"result":"{\"success\":true,\"file_path\":\"/Users/happyhawking/Documents/Projects/Current/writer/README.md\",\"content\":\"# Oral History Book Research System\\n\\nA powerful CLI tool for managing WWII oral history transcripts, designed for book research. Veterans are first-class citizens - every search, every passage, every insight stays connected to the person who lived it.\\n\\n## Table of Contents\\n\\n- [Features](#features)\\n- [Installation](#installation)\\n- [Configuration](#configuration)\\n- [Quick Start](#quick-start)\\n- [Complete Command Reference](#complete-command-reference)\\n- [Workflows](#workflows)\\n- [Understanding the System](#understanding-the-system)\\n- [Tips & Best Practices](#tips--best-practices)\\n- [Troubleshooting](#troubleshooting)\\n\\n---\\n\\n## Features\\n\\n- **Veteran-centric organization**: Veterans are characters in your book, not just metadata\\n- **LLM-powered extraction**: Automatically extracts dates, people, places, and notable stories\\n- **Narrative Synthesis**: Stepped-back analysis of a veteran's entire story and narrative potential\\n- **Topic Synthesis**: Consolidated accounts of specific historical topics across multiple veterans\\n- **Semantic + keyword search**: Find relevant passages across all testimonies using AI embeddings\\n- **Timeline synthesis**: Chronological view of events across veterans\\n- **Relationship mapping**: Track people mentioned and cross-veteran connections\\n- **Writing potential scoring**: Identifies high-value passages for your book\\n- **Chapter assignment**: Organize material by book chapter\\n- **Markdown export**: Generate research documents ready for writing\\n- **Project statistics**: Comprehensive coverage analysis and metrics\\n- **Veteran comparison**: Side-by-side analysis of accounts on the same topic\\n- **Draft generation**: AI-powered first-draft narrative text with citations\\n\\n---\\n\\n## Installation\\n\\n### Prerequisites\\n\\n- Python 3.8 or higher\\n- API keys for LLM provider (Portkey, Anthropic, or OpenAI)\\n\\n### Setup Steps\\n\\n```bash\\n# Clone or copy the project\\ncd writer\\n\\n# Create virtual environment\\npython -m venv venv\\nsource venv/bin/activate  # On Windows: venv\\\\Scripts\\\\activate\\n\\n# Install dependencies\\npip install -r requirements.txt\\n\\n# Install the CLI tool\\npip install -e .\\n```\\n\\n### Verify Installation\\n\\n```bash\\noralhistory --version\\n# Should output: 1.0.0\\n```\\n\\n---\\n\\n## Configuration\\n\\n### Environment Variables\\n\\nCreate a `.env` file in the project root with your API keys:\\n\\n```bash\\n# For Portkey (recommended)\\nPORTKEY_API_KEY=your_portkey_key_here\\nPORTKEY_MODEL=claude-sonnet-4\\nPORTKEY_EMBEDDING_MODEL=text-embedding-3-small\\n\\n# Alternative: Direct provider keys\\nANTHROPIC_API_KEY=your_key_here\\nOPENAI_API_KEY=your_key_here\\n```\\n\\n### Project Configuration\\n\\nWhen you run `oralhistory init`, a `config.yaml` file is created. You can customize:\\n\\n- **LLM Provider**: Choose between `portkey`, `anthropic`, `openai`, or `ollama`\\n- **Search Settings**: Hybrid search weights, result limits\\n- **Embedding Model**: Which model to use for semantic search\\n\\n---\\n\\n## Quick Start\\n\\n```bash\\n# 1. Initialize a new project\\noralhistory init my_book_research\\ncd my_book_research\\n\\n# 2. Ingest your first transcript\\noralhistory ingest ../transcripts/mitchell_1995.txt --veteran \\\"James Mitchell\\\"\\n\\n# 3. View the veteran profile and researcher's brief\\noralhistory veteran mitchell_james\\noralhistory profile mitchell_james\\n\\n# 4. Search for specific topics or synthesize a narrative\\noralhistory search \\\"Battle of the Bulge\\\"\\noralhistory topic \\\"Battle of the Bulge Bastogne\\\"\\n\\n# 5. View a full response\\noralhistory view mitchell_james 005\\n\\n# 6. Assign material to chapters\\noralhistory annotate mitchell_james 005 --chapter 3 --note \\\"Great opening scene\\\"\\n\\n# 7. Export for writing\\noralhistory export chapter 3\\n\\n# 8. View project statistics\\noralhistory stats\\n\\n# 9. Compare two veterans on a topic\\noralhistory compare veteran1 veteran2 --topic \\\"Bastogne\\\" --analyze\\n\\n# 10. Generate first draft\\noralhistory draft --topic \\\"tank combat\\\" --style narrative\\n```\\n\\n---\\n\\n## Complete Command Reference\\n\\n### Project Management\\n\\n#### `oralhistory init [--path PATH]`\\n\\nInitialize a new oral history research project.\\n\\n**What it does:**\\n- Creates project directory structure\\n- Generates `config.yaml` with default settings\\n- Creates folders: `veterans/`, `annotations/`, `index/`, `exports/`\\n\\n**Example:**\\n```bash\\noralhistory init my_book_research\\n# Creates: my_book_research/\\n#          \\u251c\\u2500\\u2500 config.yaml\\n#          \\u251c\\u2500\\u2500 veterans/\\n#          \\u251c\\u2500\\u2500 annotations/\\n#          \\u251c\\u2500\\u2500 index/\\n#          \\u2514\\u2500\\u2500 exports/\\n```\\n\\n**Note:** You must be in a project directory (or have one initialized) to run most other commands.\\n\\n---\\n\\n#### `oralhistory veterans`\\n\\nList all veterans in your project.\\n\\n**Output:**\\n- Table showing: ID, Name, Unit, Number of Responses\\n- Quick overview of your research corpus\\n\\n**Example:**\\n```bash\\noralhistory veterans\\n\\n# Output:\\n# \\u250c\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u252c\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u252c\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u252c\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2510\\n# \\u2502 ID                  \\u2502 Name             \\u2502 Unit                     \\u2502 Responses \\u2502\\n# \\u251c\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u253c\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u253c\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u253c\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2524\\n# \\u2502 mitchell_james      \\u2502 James Mitchell   \\u2502 101st Airborne Division  \\u2502 45        \\u2502\\n# \\u2502 smith_john          \\u2502 John Smith       \\u2502 6th Armored Division     \\u2502 38        \\u2502\\n# \\u2514\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2534\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2534\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2534\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2518\\n```\\n\\n---\\n\\n#### `oralhistory stats [--detailed]`\\n\\nShow project-wide statistics and coverage analysis.\\n\\n**What it shows:**\\n- **Summary counts**: Total veterans, responses, paragraphs, verified status, annotated responses, HIGH potential passages\\n- **Topic coverage**: Bar chart of most common topics across all veterans\\n- **Time coverage**: Bar chart showing distribution by year (1942-1945)\\n- **Top locations**: Frequency list of most mentioned locations\\n- **Writing potential distribution**: Breakdown of HIGH/MEDIUM/LOW passages with percentages\\n\\n**Options:**\\n- `--detailed`: Show per-veteran breakdown table\\n\\n**Example:**\\n```bash\\n# Basic statistics\\noralhistory stats\\n\\n# Detailed view with per-veteran stats\\noralhistory stats --detailed\\n```\\n\\n**Output includes:**\\n```\\n\\u2554\\u2550\\u2550\\u2550\\u2550\\u2550\\u2550\\u2550\\u2550\\u2550\\u2550\\u2550\\u2550\\u2550\\u2550\\u2550\\u2550\\u2550\\u2550\\u2550\\u2550\\u2550\\u2550\\u2550\\u2550\\u2550\\u2550\\u2550\\u2550\\u2550\\u2550\\u2550\\u2550\\u2550\\u2550\\u2550\\u2550\\u2550\\u2550\\u2550\\u2550\\u2550\\u2550\\u2550\\u2550\\u2550\\u2550\\u2550\\u2550\\u2550\\u2550\\u2550\\u2550\\u2550\\u2550\\u2550\\u2550\\u2550\\u2550\\u2550\\u2550\\u2550\\u2550\\u2557\\n\\u2551 PROJECT STATISTICS                                           \\u2551\\n\\u255a\\u2550\\u2550\\u2550\\u2550\\u2550\\u2550\\u2550\\u2550\\u2550\\u2550\\u2550\\u2550\\u2550\\u2550\\u2550\\u2550\\u2550\\u2550\\u2550\\u2550\\u2550\\u2550\\u2550\\u2550\\u2550\\u2550\\u2550\\u2550\\u2550\\u2550\\u2550\\u2550\\u2550\\u2550\\u2550\\u2550\\u2550\\u2550\\u2550\\u2550\\u2550\\u2550\\u2550\\u2550\\u2550\\u2550\\u2550\\u2550\\u2550\\u2550\\u2550\\u2550\\u2550\\u2550\\u2550\\u2550\\u2550\\u2550\\u2550\\u2550\\u2550\\u2550\\u255d\\n\\n Veterans:     5       Responses: 487     Paragraphs: 1,243\\n Verified:     2/5     Annotated: 156     HIGH potential: 89\\n\\n TOPIC COVERAGE\\n \\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\n combat        \\u2588\\u2588\\u2588\\u2588\\u2588\\u2588\\u2588\\u2588\\u2588\\u2588\\u2588\\u2588\\u2588\\u2588\\u2588\\u2588\\u2588\\u2588\\u2588\\u2588\\u2588\\u2588\\u2588\\u2588  142\\n training      \\u2588\\u2588\\u2588\\u2588\\u2588\\u2588\\u2588\\u2588\\u2588\\u2588\\u2588\\u2588              67\\n equipment     \\u2588\\u2588\\u2588\\u2588\\u2588\\u2588\\u2588\\u2588                  45\\n ...\\n\\n TIME COVERAGE\\n \\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\n 1942 \\u2588\\u2588\\n 1943 \\u2588\\u2588\\u2588\\u2588\\n 1944 \\u2588\\u2588\\u2588\\u2588\\u2588\\u2588\\u2588\\u2588\\u2588\\u2588\\u2588\\u2588\\u2588\\u2588\\u2588\\u2588\\u2588\\u2588\\u2588\\u2588\\u2588\\u2588\\u2588\\u2588\\u2588\\u2588\\u2588\\u2588\\u2588\\u2588\\u2588\\u2588\\n 1945 \\u2588\\u2588\\u2588\\u2588\\u2588\\u2588\\u2588\\u2588\\u2588\\u2588\\u2588\\u2588\\n```\\n\\n**Use case:** Get a bird's-eye view of your research corpus, identify coverage gaps, and see which topics are most represented.\\n\\n---\\n\\n### Ingestion\\n\\n#### `oralhistory ingest <transcript_file> --veteran \\\"Name\\\" [OPTIONS]`\\n\\nParse and ingest a transcript file into the system.\\n\\n**Required:**\\n- `transcript_file`: Path to transcript text file\\n- `--veteran, -v`: Full name of the veteran\\n\\n**Options:**\\n- `--unit, -u`: Military unit (e.g., \\\"101st Airborne Division\\\")\\n- `--rank, -r`: Military rank (e.g., \\\"Sergeant\\\", \\\"Captain\\\")\\n- `--source, -s`: Source description or location\\n- `--dry-run`: Preview what would be extracted without saving\\n- `--no-embeddings`: Skip embedding generation (faster, but no semantic search)\\n\\n**What happens:**\\n1. **Parsing**: Splits transcript into Q&A responses (handles various formats including single-line transcripts with `>>`, `-`, and named speaker markers)\\n2. **LLM Extraction**: Extracts metadata (dates, people, places, topics)\\n3. **Synthesis**: Creates timeline, relationships, notable stories\\n4. **Indexing**: Generates embeddings for semantic search\\n5. **Saving**: Creates veteran YAML file and annotation file\\n\\n**Example:**\\n```bash\\n# Full ingestion with all metadata\\noralhistory ingest transcript.txt --veteran \\\"Hugo Victor Jarussi\\\" \\\\\\n  --unit \\\"603rd Tank Destroyer Battalion\\\" \\\\\\n  --rank \\\"Sergeant\\\"\\n\\n# Quick ingestion without embeddings (faster, for testing)\\noralhistory ingest transcript.txt --veteran \\\"John Smith\\\" --no-embeddings\\n\\n# Preview what will be extracted\\noralhistory ingest transcript.txt --veteran \\\"John Smith\\\" --dry-run\\n```\\n\\n**Understanding the Output:**\\n- `\\u2713 Parsed: N responses` - How many Q&A pairs were found\\n- `\\u2713 Extracted metadata` - LLM has analyzed each response\\n- `\\u2713 Timeline: N events` - Chronological events extracted\\n- `\\u2713 Relationships: N people` - People mentioned identified\\n- `\\u2713 Notable stories: N` - High-value narrative moments flagged\\n- `\\u26a0 Verification needed` - Items that may need human review\\n\\n**Important:** If a veteran already exists, you'll be prompted to confirm overwrite. Re-ingesting will:\\n- \\u2705 Replace all responses with new parsed data\\n- \\u2705 Regenerate all embeddings\\n- \\u2705 Regenerate LLM-extracted metadata\\n- \\u2705 **Preserve** your annotations (notes, chapters, flags)\\n\\n---\\n\\n### Synthesis & Analysis\\n\\n#### `oralhistory profile <veteran_id> [--refresh]`\\n\\nGenerate and view a high-level \\\"Researcher's Brief\\\" for a single veteran.\\n\\n**What it shows:**\\n- **Overview**: A high-level summary of the veteran's story.\\n- **Narrative Style**: Analysis of how they tell their story (e.g., technical, emotional, sensory).\\n- **Thematic Strengths**: Specific areas where this veteran provides high-quality info (e.g., \\\"Combat\\\", \\\"Logistics\\\").\\n- **Writing Advice**: Specific tips on how to use this testimony in your book.\\n- **Key Entities**: Frequenty mentioned units, places, or terms.\\n\\n**Example:**\\n```bash\\noralhistory profile mitchell_james\\n```\\n\\n**Note:** The profile is automatically generated during ingestion, but you can use `--refresh` to regenerate it if you've edited the transcript or want a fresh take.\\n\\n---\\n\\n#### `oralhistory topic \\\"<query>\\\" [--limit N]`\\n\\nSynthesize a cohesive narrative on a specific topic across **all** veterans in your project.\\n\\n**What it does:**\\n1. Searches the entire project for the most relevant passages.\\n2. Uses an LLM to write a 3-5 paragraph \\\"Consolidated Account\\\".\\n3. Automatically inserts **Source Citations** (e.g., `[mitchell_james:012]`).\\n4. Highlights unique details and potential contradictions.\\n\\n**Example:**\\n```bash\\noralhistory topic \\\"C-rations and hunger\\\"\\noralhistory topic \\\"Bastogne winter conditions\\\"\\n```\\n\\n**Use case:** Perfect for the initial draft of a scene or chapter section where you need to combine multiple perspectives into one narrative.\\n\\n---\\n\\n#### `oralhistory compare <veteran1_id> <veteran2_id> --topic \\\"<query>\\\" [OPTIONS]`\\n\\nSide-by-side comparison of two veterans' accounts on the same topic.\\n\\n**What it does:**\\n1. Searches both veterans' testimonies for the specified topic\\n2. Displays matching passages in side-by-side panels\\n3. Optionally generates LLM analysis comparing their perspectives\\n\\n**Options:**\\n- `--topic, -t`: Topic to compare on (required)\\n- `--limit, -l N`: Maximum passages per veteran (default: 5)\\n- `--analyze`: Use LLM to generate comparison analysis\\n\\n**Example:**\\n```bash\\n# Basic comparison\\noralhistory compare rolfes_john_w jarussi_hugo_victor --topic \\\"Bastogne\\\"\\n\\n# With LLM analysis\\noralhistory compare rolfes_john_w pearson_john --topic \\\"training\\\" --analyze\\n\\n# Limit results\\noralhistory compare veteran1 veteran2 --topic \\\"combat\\\" --limit 3\\n```\\n\\n**Output:**\\n- Side-by-side panels showing each veteran's matching passages\\n- Response IDs and writing potential indicators\\n- LLM analysis (if `--analyze` used) highlighting:\\n  - Areas of agreement or overlapping details\\n  - Unique perspectives each veteran provides\\n  - Contradictions or different viewpoints\\n  - Which veteran provides more detail on specific aspects\\n\\n**Use case:** Compare how different veterans experienced the same event or topic. Great for identifying complementary perspectives or contradictions that need resolution.\\n\\n---\\n\\n#### `oralhistory draft [OPTIONS]`\\n\\nGenerate first-draft narrative text from selected passages with inline citations.\\n\\n**What it does:**\\n1. Collects relevant passages based on your filters\\n2. Uses LLM to weave the actual passages into narrative prose\\n3. Converts spoken language to written language while preserving authentic voice\\n4. Quotes extensively from the original accounts with inline citations like `(Rolfes, Response 014)`\\n5. Uses `(VeteranName, Response XXX, adapted)` for passages slightly edited for written flow\\n6. Displays sources used and word count\\n\\n**Options:**\\n- `--topic, -t TOPIC`: Filter by topic\\n- `--veteran, -v ID`: Filter to specific veteran\\n- `--chapter, -c N`: Filter to responses in chapter N\\n- `--query, -q QUERY`: Search query to find passages\\n- `--limit, -l N`: Maximum passages to use (default: 10)\\n- `--style, -s STYLE`: Writing style - `narrative`, `documentary`, or `academic` (default: `narrative`)\\n- `--export PATH`: Export draft to markdown file\\n\\n**Note:** You must specify at least one filter (`--topic`, `--veteran`, `--chapter`, or `--query`).\\n\\n**Examples:**\\n```bash\\n# Generate narrative draft on a topic\\noralhistory draft --topic \\\"tank combat\\\" --style narrative\\n\\n# Draft from specific veteran's chapter\\noralhistory draft --veteran rolfes_john_w --chapter 3 --style documentary\\n\\n# Draft from search results\\noralhistory draft --query \\\"Christmas 1944\\\" --limit 5 --style academic\\n\\n# Export to file\\noralhistory draft --topic \\\"training\\\" --export training_draft.md\\n```\\n\\n**Writing Styles:**\\n- **narrative**: Compelling, human narrative style with vivid scenes and sensory details\\n- **documentary**: Objective, journalistic style with extensive quotes\\n- **academic**: Scholarly style with analytical framework and formal citations\\n\\n**Output includes:**\\n- Generated draft text (500-800 words)\\n- List of all sources used with response IDs\\n- Word count\\n- Option to export to markdown file\\n\\n**Use case:** Generate first-draft prose ready for editing. Perfect for turning research into narrative text with proper citations. The LLM uses the veterans' actual words extensively, converting spoken language to written language while preserving their authentic voice. The draft tells the story through the passages themselves, not just summaries.\\n\\n**Note:** The draft tool prioritizes using the original passages. It will quote liberally from the accounts and make minimal edits to convert spoken language (removing filler words, fixing grammar) to written language while preserving the veteran's voice and specific details.\\n\\n---\\n\\n### Search & Exploration\\n\\n#### `oralhistory search <query> [OPTIONS]`\\n\\nSearch across all veterans using semantic + keyword hybrid search.\\n\\n**Options:**\\n- `--limit, -l N`: Maximum results (default: 20)\\n- `--veteran, -v ID`: Filter to specific veteran\\n- `--chapter, -c N`: Filter to responses in chapter N\\n- `--topic, -t TOPIC`: Filter by extracted topic\\n- `--potential, -p LEVEL`: Filter by writing potential (`low`, `medium`, `high`)\\n- `--export FILENAME`: Export results to markdown file\\n\\n**How it works:**\\n- Uses AI embeddings for semantic understanding\\n- Combines with keyword matching for precision\\n- Ranks by relevance score\\n- Shows truncated excerpts (use `view` for full text)\\n\\n**Examples:**\\n```bash\\n# Basic search\\noralhistory search \\\"tank destroyer\\\"\\n\\n# Find high-potential passages about a specific topic\\noralhistory search \\\"food rations hardship\\\" --potential high\\n\\n# Search within one veteran's testimony\\noralhistory search \\\"training\\\" --veteran mitchell_james\\n\\n# Find material already assigned to a chapter\\noralhistory search \\\"combat\\\" --chapter 5\\n\\n# Export search results for later review\\noralhistory search \\\"Battle of the Bulge\\\" --export bulge_research.md\\n```\\n\\n**Understanding Results:**\\n- Results grouped by veteran\\n- Each result shows: Response ID, Chapter (if assigned), Writing Potential\\n- Truncated question and answer (use `view` for full text)\\n- Your notes and flags are displayed\\n- LLM summary and writing notes included\\n\\n---\\n\\n#### `oralhistory veteran <veteran_id> [OPTIONS]`\\n\\nShow detailed profile of a single veteran.\\n\\n**Options:**\\n- `--timeline`: Show only timeline\\n- `--relationships`: Show only relationships\\n- `--responses`: Show all responses (list view)\\n- `--response ID`: Show specific response in detail\\n\\n**Default view shows:**\\n- Basic info (name, unit, rank, service dates)\\n- Timeline (chronological events)\\n- Relationships (people mentioned)\\n- Notable stories (high-value passages)\\n- Verification items (things needing review)\\n\\n**Examples:**\\n```bash\\n# Full profile\\noralhistory veteran mitchell_james\\n\\n# Just the timeline\\noralhistory veteran mitchell_james --timeline\\n\\n# Just relationships\\noralhistory veteran mitchell_james --relationships\\n\\n# List all responses\\noralhistory veteran mitchell_james --responses\\n\\n# View specific response\\noralhistory veteran mitchell_james --response 005\\n```\\n\\n**Understanding Verification Items:**\\n- These are flags the LLM creates when it detects potential issues\\n- Common types: Name discrepancies, timeline precision, unit details, geographic precision\\n- Each item may have `response_ids` showing which responses are related\\n- **What to do:**\\n  - If metadata is correct \\u2192 Remove the item from YAML or ignore\\n  - If metadata is wrong \\u2192 Fix it, then remove the item\\n  - If uncertain \\u2192 Add annotation note and investigate\\n  - **New:** Use `oralhistory verify <veteran_id>` for an interactive wizard to resolve these items.\\n\\n---\\n\\n#### `oralhistory verify <veteran_id>`\\n\\nInteractive wizard to audit and correct a veteran's data.\\n\\n**What it does:**\\n1. **Identity Check**: Confirm or edit Name, Rank, and Unit.\\n2. **Timeline Audit**: Review events with low confidence or notes.\\n3. **Flag Resolution**: Walk through every \\\"Verification Needed\\\" item.\\n4. **Auto-Reindex**: Automatically updates the search index if you make changes.\\n\\n**Example:**\\n```bash\\noralhistory verify mitchell_james\\n```\\n\\n**Workflow:**\\n- Run this after `oralhistory ingest` to ensure your data is 100% accurate.\\n- Use `[v]iew sources` during the wizard to see the original transcript passages related to a flag.\\n\\n---\\n\\n#### `oralhistory view <veteran_id> <response_id>`\\n\\nView the **complete, untruncated** text of a specific response.\\n\\n**What it shows:**\\n- Full interviewer question (no truncation)\\n- Full veteran answer (no truncation)\\n- Extracted metadata (summary, topics, locations, people, dates)\\n- Writing potential and LLM notes\\n- Your annotations (notes, flags, chapter assignment)\\n\\n**Use cases:**\\n- After finding interesting results in search\\n- When reviewing verification items\\n- Before assigning to chapters\\n- When writing and need full context\\n\\n**Example:**\\n```bash\\n# View response 005 from mitchell_james\\noralhistory view mitchell_james 005\\n\\n# After a search, view the full passage\\noralhistory search \\\"tank destroyer\\\"\\n# ... see interesting result [012] ...\\noralhistory view mitchell_james 012\\n```\\n\\n**Quick Actions Shown:**\\nThe view command displays hints for common follow-up actions:\\n- Assign to chapter\\n- Add note\\n- Export veteran\\n\\n---\\n\\n### Annotations\\n\\n#### `oralhistory annotate <veteran_id> [response_id] [OPTIONS]`\\n\\nAdd annotations to responses or veterans.\\n\\n**Response-level annotations:**\\n- `--note, -n \\\"text\\\"`: Add a note to a specific response\\n- `--chapter, -c N`: Assign response to chapter N\\n- `--flag FLAG`: Add a custom flag (e.g., \\\"needs-verification\\\", \\\"use-for-opening\\\")\\n- `--remove-flag FLAG`: Remove a flag\\n\\n**Veteran-level annotations:**\\n- `--veteran-note \\\"text\\\"`: Add note about the veteran (not specific to a response)\\n- `--todo \\\"text\\\"`: Add research todo item\\n\\n**Examples:**\\n```bash\\n# Add a note to a response\\noralhistory annotate mitchell_james 005 --note \\\"Great opening scene - use this\\\"\\n\\n# Assign to chapter\\noralhistory annotate mitchell_james 005 --chapter 3\\n\\n# Add multiple annotations at once\\noralhistory annotate mitchell_james 005 \\\\\\n  --chapter 3 \\\\\\n  --note \\\"Opening scene\\\" \\\\\\n  --flag \\\"high-priority\\\"\\n\\n# Add veteran-level note\\noralhistory annotate mitchell_james --veteran-note \\\"Introduce in chapter 2\\\"\\n\\n# Add research todo\\noralhistory annotate mitchell_james --todo \\\"Verify unit movements against history\\\"\\n\\n# Remove a flag\\noralhistory annotate mitchell_james 005 --remove-flag \\\"needs-verification\\\"\\n```\\n\\n**Best Practices:**\\n- Use flags for workflow states: `needs-verification`, `fact-checked`, `used-in-draft`\\n- Use notes for specific writing guidance\\n- Use chapters to organize material as you research\\n- Veteran notes are great for character development ideas\\n\\n---\\n\\n#### `oralhistory annotations [veteran_id] [OPTIONS]`\\n\\nView annotations.\\n\\n**Options:**\\n- `veteran_id`: Show annotations for specific veteran\\n- `--chapter, -c N`: Show all responses assigned to chapter N\\n- `--flags`: Show all flagged responses across all veterans\\n\\n**Examples:**\\n```bash\\n# View all annotations for a veteran\\noralhistory annotations mitchell_james\\n\\n# See what's assigned to chapter 5\\noralhistory annotations --chapter 5\\n\\n# Find all flagged responses\\noralhistory annotations --flags\\n```\\n\\n---\\n\\n### Chapter Management\\n\\n#### `oralhistory chapter <number> [--export]`\\n\\nView or export material assigned to a chapter.\\n\\n**What it shows:**\\n- All responses assigned to the chapter\\n- Grouped by veteran\\n- Your notes for each response\\n- Writing potential indicators\\n\\n**Options:**\\n- `--export`: Export chapter to markdown file\\n\\n**Example:**\\n```bash\\n# View chapter 3\\noralhistory chapter 3\\n\\n# Export chapter 3 for writing\\noralhistory chapter 3 --export\\n# Creates: exports/chapter_03.md\\n```\\n\\n**Use case:** This is your primary tool for organizing material as you research. Assign responses to chapters as you discover them, then export when ready to write.\\n\\n---\\n\\n### Export\\n\\n#### `oralhistory export chapter <number> [--filename FILE]`\\n\\nExport a chapter to markdown.\\n\\n**What's included:**\\n- All responses assigned to the chapter\\n- Full text (not truncated)\\n- Your annotations and notes\\n- Extracted metadata\\n- Organized by veteran\\n\\n**Example:**\\n```bash\\noralhistory export chapter 3\\n# Creates: exports/chapter_03.md\\n\\noralhistory export chapter 3 --filename my_chapter.md\\n# Creates: exports/my_chapter.md\\n```\\n\\n---\\n\\n#### `oralhistory export veteran <veteran_id> [--filename FILE]`\\n\\nExport all material for a veteran.\\n\\n**What's included:**\\n- Full profile (timeline, relationships, notable stories)\\n- All responses with full text\\n- Your annotations\\n- Verification items\\n- Ready for character development or fact-checking\\n\\n**Example:**\\n```bash\\noralhistory export veteran mitchell_james\\n# Creates: exports/mitchell_james.md\\n```\\n\\n---\\n\\n#### `oralhistory export stories [--filename FILE]`\\n\\nExport all notable stories across all veterans.\\n\\n**What's included:**\\n- All stories flagged as high writing potential\\n- Organized by veteran\\n- Response references\\n- LLM writing notes\\n\\n**Use case:** Quick way to find the best narrative moments across your entire corpus.\\n\\n**Example:**\\n```bash\\noralhistory export stories\\n# Creates: exports/notable_stories.md\\n```\\n\\n---\\n\\n#### `oralhistory export timeline [--start YYYY-MM] [--end YYYY-MM] [--filename FILE]`\\n\\nExport combined timeline across all veterans.\\n\\n**Options:**\\n- `--start`: Filter to events after this date\\n- `--end`: Filter to events before this date\\n\\n**What's included:**\\n- Chronological list of all events\\n- Grouped by date\\n- Shows which veteran(s) mentioned each event\\n- Response references for verification\\n\\n**Example:**\\n```bash\\n# Full timeline\\noralhistory export timeline\\n\\n# Timeline for specific period\\noralhistory export timeline --start 1944-06 --end 1944-12\\n# Creates: exports/timeline_1944-06_to_1944-12.md\\n```\\n\\n---\\n\\n### Maintenance\\n\\n#### `oralhistory reindex [--no-embeddings]`\\n\\nRebuild search indexes for all veterans.\\n\\n**When to use:**\\n- After manually editing veteran YAML files\\n- If search seems broken or incomplete\\n- After updating embeddings model\\n- If you want to regenerate embeddings without re-ingesting\\n\\n**Options:**\\n- `--no-embeddings`: Skip embedding generation (faster, but semantic search won't work)\\n\\n**Example:**\\n```bash\\n# Full reindex with embeddings\\noralhistory reindex\\n\\n# Reindex structure only (no API calls)\\noralhistory reindex --no-embeddings\\n```\\n\\n**Note:** This preserves your annotations. Only the search index is rebuilt.\\n\\n---\\n\\n## Workflows\\n\\n### Workflow 1: Initial Research Phase\\n\\n**Goal:** Ingest multiple transcripts and get overview\\n\\n```bash\\n# 1. Initialize project\\noralhistory init my_book\\ncd my_book\\n\\n# 2. Ingest multiple transcripts\\noralhistory ingest transcript1.txt --veteran \\\"James Mitchell\\\"\\noralhistory ingest transcript2.txt --veteran \\\"John Smith\\\"\\noralhistory ingest transcript3.txt --veteran \\\"Robert Jones\\\"\\n\\n# 3. Get overview\\noralhistory veterans  # See who you have\\noralhistory veteran mitchell_james  # Review each profile\\n\\n# 4. Find notable stories\\noralhistory export stories  # See best narrative moments\\n\\n# 5. Search for themes\\noralhistory search \\\"combat experience\\\" --potential high\\noralhistory search \\\"daily life\\\" --potential high\\n```\\n\\n---\\n\\n### Workflow 2: Organizing Material by Chapter\\n\\n**Goal:** Assign material to chapters as you research\\n\\n```bash\\n# 1. Search for material relevant to chapter 3\\noralhistory search \\\"training camp\\\" --potential high\\n\\n# 2. Review full passages\\noralhistory view mitchell_james 012\\noralhistory view smith_john 005\\n\\n# 3. Assign to chapter with notes\\noralhistory annotate mitchell_james 012 --chapter 3 --note \\\"Opening scene\\\"\\noralhistory annotate smith_john 005 --chapter 3 --note \\\"Supporting detail\\\"\\n\\n# 4. Review chapter organization\\noralhistory chapter 3\\n\\n# 5. Export when ready to write\\noralhistory export chapter 3\\n```\\n\\n---\\n\\n### Workflow 3: Character Development\\n\\n**Goal:** Deep dive into a single veteran's story\\n\\n```bash\\n# 1. View full profile\\noralhistory veteran mitchell_james\\n\\n# 2. Review timeline\\noralhistory veteran mitchell_james --timeline\\n\\n# 3. See relationships\\noralhistory veteran mitchell_james --relationships\\n\\n# 4. Read notable stories\\n# (shown in profile, or export stories)\\n\\n# 5. Export for character work\\noralhistory export veteran mitchell_james\\n\\n# 6. Add character notes\\noralhistory annotate mitchell_james --veteran-note \\\"Strong voice, use for POV character\\\"\\n```\\n\\n---\\n\\n### Workflow 4: Fact-Checking & Verification\\n\\n**Goal:** Verify information and resolve discrepancies\\n\\n```bash\\n# 1. Check verification items\\noralhistory veteran mitchell_james\\n# Look for \\\"VERIFICATION NEEDED\\\" section\\n\\n# 2. View related responses\\noralhistory view mitchell_james 001  # If response_ids shown\\n\\n# 3. Add verification notes\\noralhistory annotate mitchell_james 001 --note \\\"Verified: Name correct as James Mitchell\\\"\\noralhistory annotate mitchell_james 001 --flag \\\"fact-checked\\\"\\n\\n# 4. Find all unverified items\\noralhistory annotations --flags | grep -v fact-checked\\n\\n# 5. Export for external verification\\noralhistory export veteran mitchell_james\\n```\\n\\n---\\n\\n### Workflow 5: Writing Phase\\n\\n**Goal:** Generate material ready for writing\\n\\n```bash\\n# 1. Review chapter organization\\noralhistory chapter 5\\n\\n# 2. Export chapter\\noralhistory export chapter 5\\n# Opens exports/chapter_05.md in your editor\\n\\n# 3. As you write, flag used material\\noralhistory annotate mitchell_james 012 --flag \\\"used-in-draft\\\"\\n\\n# 4. Find additional material if needed\\noralhistory search \\\"specific detail\\\" --chapter 5\\n\\n# 5. Export timeline for chronological reference\\noralhistory export timeline --start 1944-06 --end 1944-12\\n```\\n\\n---\\n\\n### Workflow 6: Narrative Development\\n\\n**Goal:** Step back from details to build your book's narrative arc\\n\\n```bash\\n# 1. Get a researcher's brief for your main character\\noralhistory profile jarussi_hugo_victor\\n\\n# 2. Synthesize a specific historical event\\noralhistory topic \\\"crossing the Rhine pontoon bridge\\\"\\n\\n# 3. Compare perspectives on a specific hardship\\noralhistory topic \\\"winter 1944 cold and equipment\\\"\\n\\n# 4. Use synthesized accounts as a first draft or outline\\n# (Topic synthesis provides grounded citations for easy verification)\\n```\\n\\n---\\n\\n### Workflow 7: Project Overview & Statistics\\n\\n**Goal:** Get a comprehensive view of your research corpus\\n\\n```bash\\n# 1. View project statistics\\noralhistory stats\\n\\n# 2. See detailed breakdown\\noralhistory stats --detailed\\n\\n# 3. Compare two veterans on a topic\\noralhistory compare rolfes_john_w jarussi_hugo_victor --topic \\\"Bastogne\\\" --analyze\\n\\n# 4. Generate first draft from research\\noralhistory draft --topic \\\"tank combat\\\" --style narrative --export draft.md\\n```\\n\\n---\\n\\n### Workflow 8: Draft Generation\\n\\n**Goal:** Turn research into first-draft narrative prose\\n\\n```bash\\n# 1. Generate draft from topic search\\noralhistory draft --topic \\\"Christmas 1944\\\" --style narrative\\n\\n# 2. Generate draft from chapter material\\noralhistory draft --chapter 5 --style documentary\\n\\n# 3. Generate draft from specific veteran\\noralhistory draft --veteran rolfes_john_w --limit 10 --style narrative\\n\\n# 4. Export for editing\\noralhistory draft --query \\\"training camp\\\" --export training_draft.md\\n```\\n\\n---\\n\\n## Understanding the System\\n\\n### Data Model\\n\\n**Veteran** (Primary Entity)\\n- The central organizing principle\\n- Contains: Basic info, timeline, relationships, notable stories, responses\\n- Stored in: `veterans/<veteran_id>.yaml`\\n\\n**Response** (Atomic Unit)\\n- One interviewer question + veteran's complete answer\\n- Contains: Full text, extracted metadata, writing potential score\\n- Identified by: `<veteran_id>:<response_id>` (e.g., `mitchell_james:005`)\\n\\n**Paragraph** (Search Precision)\\n- Subdivisions of responses for precise search\\n- Used internally for embedding generation\\n\\n**Narrative Synthesis** (High-Level View)\\n- **Veteran Profile**: A \\\"briefing\\\" on one veteran's strengths and style.\\n- **Topic Synthesis**: A \\\"consolidated account\\\" of a topic across multiple veterans.\\n- These layers help prevent \\\"detail loss\\\" by providing a wide-angle lens on your research.\\n\\n**Annotations** (Your Layer)\\n- Your notes, chapter assignments, flags, todos\\n- Stored separately in: `annotations/<veteran_id>.yaml`\\n- **Preserved** when re-ingesting transcripts\\n\\n### Writing Potential Scores\\n\\nThe LLM scores each response:\\n\\n- **HIGH** \\u2b50: Vivid sensory detail, action sequences, named characters, emotional moments, unique perspectives\\n- **MEDIUM**: Useful context, fills gaps, corroborates other accounts\\n- **LOW**: Routine biographical info, vague recollections, administrative details\\n\\n**Use `--potential high` in search** to find the best material quickly.\\n\\n### Search Technology\\n\\n- **Semantic Search**: Uses AI embeddings to understand meaning, not just keywords\\n- **Keyword Search**: Traditional text matching for precision\\n- **Hybrid**: Combines both (default: 70% semantic, 30% keyword)\\n- **Embeddings**: Generated using Portkey/OpenAI embeddings API\\n- **Storage**: SQLite database with vector similarity search\\n\\n### File Structure\\n\\n```\\nmy_book_research/\\n\\u251c\\u2500\\u2500 config.yaml              # Project configuration\\n\\u251c\\u2500\\u2500 veterans/                # Veteran data (auto-generated)\\n\\u2502   \\u2514\\u2500\\u2500 mitchell_james.yaml\\n\\u251c\\u2500\\u2500 annotations/             # Your notes (preserved on re-ingest)\\n\\u2502   \\u2514\\u2500\\u2500 mitchell_james.yaml\\n\\u251c\\u2500\\u2500 index/                   # Search database\\n\\u2502   \\u2514\\u2500\\u2500 embeddings.sqlite\\n\\u2514\\u2500\\u2500 exports/                 # Generated markdown files\\n    \\u251c\\u2500\\u2500 chapter_03.md\\n    \\u2514\\u2500\\u2500 mitchell_james.md\\n```\\n\\n**Important:** \\n- `veterans/` files are regenerated on re-ingest\\n- `annotations/` files are **preserved** on re-ingest\\n- `index/` is rebuilt with `reindex` command\\n- `exports/` can be deleted and regenerated anytime\\n\\n---\\n\\n## Tips & Best Practices\\n\\n### 1. Start Broad, Then Narrow\\n\\n- Ingest multiple transcripts before diving deep\\n- Use `oralhistory veterans` to get overview\\n- Use `oralhistory export stories` to find best moments\\n- Then focus on specific veterans or chapters\\n\\n### 2. Use Writing Potential Scores\\n\\n- Filter searches with `--potential high` to find best material\\n- Notable stories are pre-flagged as high potential\\n- Don't ignore medium/low - they provide context\\n\\n### 3. Organize as You Research\\n\\n- Assign responses to chapters as you discover them\\n- Add notes immediately while context is fresh\\n- Use flags for workflow: `needs-verification`, `fact-checked`, `used-in-draft`\\n\\n### 4. Leverage Search Power\\n\\n- Use natural language queries: \\\"food shortages during winter\\\"\\n- Combine filters: `--potential high --chapter 5`\\n- Export search results for later review\\n\\n### 5. Export Early, Export Often\\n\\n- Generate chapter exports as you assign material\\n- Export veterans for character development work\\n- Export timeline for chronological reference\\n- Exports are markdown - edit them directly if needed\\n\\n### 6. Handle Verification Items\\n\\n- Review verification items after ingestion\\n- If metadata is correct \\u2192 Remove from YAML or ignore\\n- If metadata is wrong \\u2192 Fix it, then remove item\\n- Add annotation notes to document your verification\\n\\n### 7. Use Git for Version Control\\n\\n```bash\\ngit init\\ngit add .\\ngit commit -m \\\"Initial ingestion: 3 veterans\\\"\\n# Commit after each research session\\n```\\n\\n### 8. Re-ingesting Transcripts\\n\\n**What happens:**\\n- \\u2705 Responses replaced with new parsed data\\n- \\u2705 Embeddings regenerated\\n- \\u2705 LLM metadata regenerated\\n- \\u2705 **Your annotations preserved**\\n\\n**Best practice:**\\n- Use `--dry-run` first to preview\\n- Export important data before re-ingesting\\n- Consider manual edits vs. re-ingest trade-offs\\n\\n### 9. Performance Tips\\n\\n- Use `--no-embeddings` for quick testing\\n- Reindex only when needed\\n- Search is fast once indexed\\n- Large projects (100+ veterans) work fine\\n\\n### 10. Troubleshooting Workflow\\n\\n1. **Search not finding things?** \\u2192 Run `oralhistory reindex`\\n2. **Wrong data?** \\u2192 Check verification items, edit YAML manually\\n3. **Missing responses?** \\u2192 Check transcript format, parser may need adjustment\\n4. **API errors?** \\u2192 Check `.env` file, verify API keys\\n\\n---\\n\\n## Troubleshooting\\n\\n### \\\"Not in an oralhistory project\\\"\\n\\n**Problem:** Command fails with this error.\\n\\n**Solution:**\\n```bash\\n# Make sure you're in a project directory\\ncd my_book_research\\n\\n# Or initialize a new project\\noralhistory init .\\n```\\n\\n---\\n\\n### \\\"Veteran not found\\\"\\n\\n**Problem:** Can't find veteran by ID.\\n\\n**Solution:**\\n```bash\\n# List all veterans to see correct IDs\\noralhistory veterans\\n\\n# IDs are generated from names: \\\"James Mitchell\\\" \\u2192 \\\"mitchell_james\\\"\\n# Use the exact ID shown in the list\\n```\\n\\n---\\n\\n### Search Returns No Results\\n\\n**Problem:** Search finds nothing even when you know material exists.\\n\\n**Solutions:**\\n1. **Check if embeddings were generated:**\\n   ```bash\\n   # Reindex to regenerate embeddings\\n   oralhistory reindex\\n   ```\\n\\n2. **Try different search terms:**\\n   ```bash\\n   # More specific\\n   oralhistory search \\\"tank destroyer battalion\\\"\\n   \\n   # More general\\n   oralhistory search \\\"tank\\\"\\n   ```\\n\\n3. **Check filters:**\\n   ```bash\\n   # Remove filters that might be too restrictive\\n   oralhistory search \\\"combat\\\"  # Without --chapter or --potential\\n   ```\\n\\n---\\n\\n### Verification Items with Empty Details\\n\\n**Problem:** Verification items show but have no detail text.\\n\\n**Explanation:** The LLM flagged an issue but didn't provide specifics. This often happens with general transcript-level concerns.\\n\\n**What to do:**\\n- If metadata looks correct \\u2192 Ignore or remove from YAML\\n- If uncertain \\u2192 Review the transcript manually\\n- Check `response_ids` to see which responses are related\\n\\n---\\n\\n### Transcript Parses as Single Response\\n\\n**Problem:** Ingest shows \\\"Parsed: 1 responses\\\" when there should be many.\\n\\n**Causes:**\\n- Transcript is all on one line (no line breaks)\\n- Missing Q:/A: markers or speaker names\\n- Parser can't detect speaker patterns\\n\\n**Solutions:**\\n1. **The parser now handles single-line transcripts automatically** and recognizes:\\n   - `>>` chevron markers (converts to alternating Q:/A:)\\n   - `- Question? -` wrapped questions (converts to Q: format)\\n   - Named speakers like `HURST:`, `HALL:`, `Heldreth:` (adds newlines)\\n2. **Format transcript with Q:/A: markers:**\\n   ```\\n   Q: Question text here?\\n   \\n   A: Answer text here.\\n   \\n   Q: Next question?\\n   \\n   A: Next answer.\\n   ```\\n3. **Check transcript format** - parser looks for:\\n   - `Q:` / `A:` markers\\n   - `Interviewer:` / `Veteran:` markers\\n   - Speaker names followed by colons\\n   - `>>` chevron markers\\n   - `- Question? -` wrapped questions\\n\\n---\\n\\n### API Key Errors\\n\\n**Problem:** \\\"API key not set\\\" or authentication errors.\\n\\n**Solutions:**\\n1. **Check `.env` file exists and has correct keys:**\\n   ```bash\\n   cat .env\\n   # Should show: PORTKEY_API_KEY=... or ANTHROPIC_API_KEY=...\\n   ```\\n\\n2. **Verify keys are valid:**\\n   - Test with provider's API directly\\n   - Check for typos or extra spaces\\n\\n3. **Use correct environment variable names:**\\n   - Portkey: `PORTKEY_API_KEY`, `PORTKEY_MODEL`, `PORTKEY_EMBEDDING_MODEL`\\n   - Anthropic: `ANTHROPIC_API_KEY`\\n   - OpenAI: `OPENAI_API_KEY`\\n\\n---\\n\\n### Embedding Generation Fails\\n\\n**Problem:** Embeddings fail during ingest or reindex.\\n\\n**Solutions:**\\n1. **Skip embeddings temporarily:**\\n   ```bash\\n   oralhistory ingest transcript.txt --veteran \\\"Name\\\" --no-embeddings\\n   # Then reindex later when API is working\\n   ```\\n\\n2. **Check API quota/limits:**\\n   - Verify you have API credits\\n   - Check rate limits\\n\\n3. **Regenerate later:**\\n   ```bash\\n   oralhistory reindex  # Regenerates all embeddings\\n   ```\\n\\n---\\n\\n### Re-ingest Lost My Annotations\\n\\n**Problem:** After re-ingesting, annotations are gone.\\n\\n**This shouldn't happen!** Annotations are stored separately and preserved.\\n\\n**If it happened:**\\n1. Check `annotations/` directory - files should still exist\\n2. Annotations are in separate YAML files, not in veteran files\\n3. If truly lost, check git history or backups\\n\\n**Prevention:**\\n- Commit annotations to git before re-ingesting\\n- Export important annotations before major changes\\n\\n---\\n\\n### Chapter Export Missing Responses\\n\\n**Problem:** Chapter export doesn't include all assigned responses.\\n\\n**Solutions:**\\n1. **Verify assignments:**\\n   ```bash\\n   oralhistory annotations --chapter 5\\n   # Should show all responses assigned to chapter 5\\n   ```\\n\\n2. **Check response IDs match:**\\n   - Response IDs are like `001`, `002`, etc.\\n   - Must match exactly (case-sensitive)\\n\\n3. **Re-export:**\\n   ```bash\\n   oralhistory export chapter 5\\n   ```\\n\\n---\\n\\n## Advanced Usage\\n\\n### Manual YAML Editing\\n\\nYou can edit veteran YAML files directly:\\n\\n```bash\\n# Edit veteran file\\nvim veterans/mitchell_james.yaml\\n\\n# After editing, reindex to update search\\noralhistory reindex\\n```\\n\\n**What you can safely edit:**\\n- Basic info (name, unit, rank) - will be overwritten on re-ingest\\n- Timeline events - can add/remove\\n- Relationships - can modify\\n- Verification items - can remove resolved items\\n\\n**What to avoid:**\\n- Response IDs - must match annotations\\n- Response structure - breaking this breaks search\\n\\n### Custom Flags Workflow\\n\\nUse flags for your own workflow:\\n\\n```bash\\n# Mark for fact-checking\\noralhistory annotate mitchell_james 005 --flag \\\"needs-fact-check\\\"\\n\\n# Mark as verified\\noralhistory annotate mitchell_james 005 --flag \\\"fact-checked\\\"\\n\\n# Mark as used in draft\\noralhistory annotate mitchell_james 005 --flag \\\"used-in-draft\\\"\\n\\n# Find all unverified\\noralhistory annotations --flags | grep needs-fact-check\\n```\\n\\n### Batch Operations\\n\\nUse shell scripts for batch operations:\\n\\n```bash\\n# Ingest multiple transcripts\\nfor file in transcripts/*.txt; do\\n  name=$(basename \\\"$file\\\" .txt)\\n  oralhistory ingest \\\"$file\\\" --veteran \\\"$name\\\"\\ndone\\n\\n# Export all chapters\\nfor i in {1..10}; do\\n  oralhistory export chapter $i\\ndone\\n```\\n\\n---\\n\\n## License\\n\\nMIT License\\n\\n---\\n\\n## Getting Help\\n\\n- Check this README first\\n- Review command help: `oralhistory <command> --help`\\n- Check `specs.md` for technical details\\n- Review example outputs in terminal\\n\\n---\\n\\n**Happy researching! \\ud83c\\udf96\\ufe0f**\\n\",\"file_size_bytes\":40721,\"content_length\":39350}"},"isError":false}} { metadata: undefined }
