2025-12-20T17:19:59.248Z [iterm2] [info] Message from client: {"method":"tools/call","params":{"name":"write_file","arguments":{"file_path":"/Users/happyhawking/Documents/Projects/Current/writer/README.md","content":"# Super Sixth\n\n**A research engine for oral history book projects.**\n\nYou have 50-100 veteran interviews. Hours of transcripts. Names, dates, places, stories — scattered across thousands of pages. You need to write a book.\n\nThis tool helps you find what you're looking for, understand who said what, and never lose the thread back to the person who lived it.\n\n---\n\n## The Problem\n\nOral history research is brutal. You're drowning in material. Every veteran tells their story differently — some ramble, some are precise, some remember smells and sounds, others remember dates and unit numbers.\n\nTraditional approaches fail:\n- **Ctrl+F** finds words, not meaning. Searching \"cold\" won't find the guy describing \"frost on the inside of the tank.\"\n- **Folders and tags** separate the story from the storyteller. You lose context.\n- **Spreadsheets** turn living memory into dead data.\n\nYou need a system that treats **veterans as first-class citizens** — where every quote, every insight, every detail stays connected to the person who said it.\n\n---\n\n## The Solution\n\nSuper Sixth gives you four ways to interrogate your research, each designed for a different question:\n\n### 1. The Fact-Checker (Semantic Search)\n*\"Find me passages about hunger, even if they don't use that word.\"*\n\n```bash\noralhistory search \"food shortages hunger\"\n```\n\nThis uses AI embeddings to find passages by *meaning*, not just keywords. It finds the guy talking about \"trading cigarettes for bread\" when you search for \"hunger.\"\n\n### 2. The Historian (Timeline & Metadata)\n*\"Show me everything this veteran experienced, in order.\"*\n\n```bash\noralhistory veteran rolfes_john --timeline\n```\n\nSee one person's war chronologically. When did they ship out? Where were they in December '44? What happened after the crossing? The system extracts dates and builds timelines automatically.\n\n### 3. The Journalist (Context Windows)\n*\"I found a great quote. What came before and after it?\"*\n\n```bash\noralhistory context rolfes_john 014 --before 2 --after 2\n```\n\nYou found response #14 in a search. But quotes don't exist in isolation. This shows you the surrounding conversation — what question prompted it, what they said next, the full story around the soundbite.\n\n### 4. The Cross-Reference (Entity Search)\n*\"Who else mentions General Gruber? Do their stories match?\"*\n\n```bash\noralhistory entity \"Captain Gruber\"\noralhistory entity \"Bastogne\" --type location\n```\n\nFind the same person, place, or unit mentioned across *different* veterans. This is how you spot contradictions, corroborate details, and build a complete picture from fragments.\n\n---\n\n## What Makes This Different\n\n**Veterans are characters, not folders.** Every search result shows who said it. Every export groups by storyteller. The system knows that \"Mitchell\" and \"Lt. Mitchell\" and \"James Mitchell\" might be the same person.\n\n**Responses are atomic.** One question + one answer = one unit. You can annotate it, assign it to a chapter, flag it for fact-checking. That annotation follows it everywhere.\n\n**Your notes survive.** Re-ingest a transcript with better parsing? Your chapter assignments and notes are preserved. The system separates *their* data from *your* work.\n\n**Writing potential is scored.** The AI reads every response and flags the gems — vivid sensory detail, named characters, emotional moments, action sequences. Filter by `--potential high` to find your book's best material fast.\n\n---\n\n## Quick Start\n\n```bash\n# Initialize a project\noralhistory init mybook\ncd mybook\n\n# Ingest your first transcript\noralhistory ingest ../transcripts/rolfes_1995.txt \\\n  --veteran \"John W. Rolfes\" \\\n  --unit \"6th Armored Division\"\n\n# See what you've got\noralhistory veteran rolfes_john_w\n\n# Find the good stuff\noralhistory search \"combat\" --potential high\n\n# Read the full passage\noralhistory view rolfes_john_w 023\n\n# Assign it to your chapter\noralhistory annotate rolfes_john_w 023 --chapter 3 --note \"Opening scene\"\n```\n\n---\n\n## The Commands\n\n### Getting Started\n\n| Command | What it does |\n|---------|--------------|\n| `oralhistory init` | Create a new project |\n| `oralhistory ingest <file> --veteran \"Name\"` | Parse and import a transcript |\n| `oralhistory veterans` | List everyone in your project |\n\n### Finding Material\n\n| Command | What it does |\n|---------|--------------|\n| `oralhistory search \"query\"` | Semantic search across all veterans |\n| `oralhistory entity \"Patton\"` | Find who mentions a specific person/place/unit |\n| `oralhistory topic \"Bastogne\"` | AI-synthesized narrative across all veterans |\n\n**Search examples:**\n```bash\n# Find passages about tank combat\noralhistory search \"tank combat destroyed\"\n\n# Find only high-potential passages\noralhistory search \"winter cold\" --potential high\n\n# Search within one veteran\noralhistory search \"training\" --veteran rolfes_john_w\n\n# Find everyone who mentions a specific person\noralhistory entity \"Gruber\"\n\n# Find all location references\noralhistory entity \"Rhine\" --type location\n```\n\n### Understanding a Veteran\n\n| Command | What it does |\n|---------|--------------|\n| `oralhistory veteran <id>` | Full profile: timeline, relationships, notable stories |\n| `oralhistory veteran <id> --timeline` | Just the chronology |\n| `oralhistory profile <id>` | AI-generated \"researcher's brief\" |\n| `oralhistory view <id> <response>` | Read a full response untruncated |\n| `oralhistory context <id> <response>` | See surrounding Q&A for context |\n\n**Example workflow:**\n```bash\n# Get overview of this veteran\noralhistory veteran jarussi_hugo\n\n# See their chronological story\noralhistory veteran jarussi_hugo --timeline\n\n# Get the AI's take on their narrative strengths\noralhistory profile jarussi_hugo\n\n# Found interesting response #14 in search? Read the full thing\noralhistory view jarussi_hugo 014\n\n# What led to that answer? What came next?\noralhistory context jarussi_hugo 014 --before 2 --after 2\n```\n\n### Organizing Your Research\n\n| Command | What it does |\n|---------|--------------|\n| `oralhistory annotate <id> <resp> --chapter N` | Assign response to a chapter |\n| `oralhistory annotate <id> <resp> --note \"text\"` | Add your notes |\n| `oralhistory annotate <id> <resp> --flag \"name\"` | Add a custom flag |\n| `oralhistory chapter N` | See everything assigned to chapter N |\n| `oralhistory annotations --chapter N` | List all chapter N annotations |\n\n**Example workflow:**\n```bash\n# Found a great passage — assign it\noralhistory annotate rolfes_john_w 023 \\\n  --chapter 3 \\\n  --note \"Great opening — sensory detail about the cold\"\n\n# Later, review your chapter material\noralhistory chapter 3\n\n# Find all passages you've flagged\noralhistory annotations --flags\n```\n\n### Synthesis & Writing\n\n| Command | What it does |\n|---------|--------------|\n| `oralhistory topic \"subject\"` | AI narrative synthesizing all veterans on a topic |\n| `oralhistory compare <vet1> <vet2> --topic \"X\"` | Side-by-side comparison |\n| `oralhistory draft --topic \"X\"` | Generate first-draft prose with citations |\n\n**Example workflow:**\n```bash\n# Get a synthesized narrative about Bastogne across all veterans\noralhistory topic \"Bastogne December 1944\"\n\n# Compare two veterans' accounts\noralhistory compare rolfes_john_w jarussi_hugo --topic \"crossing the Rhine\" --analyze\n\n# Generate a first draft with inline citations\noralhistory draft --topic \"tank combat\" --style narrative --export draft.md\n```\n\n### Exporting\n\n| Command | What it does |\n|---------|--------------|\n| `oralhistory export chapter N` | Export chapter to markdown |\n| `oralhistory export veteran <id>` | Export one veteran's full material |\n| `oralhistory export stories` | Export all high-potential passages |\n| `oralhistory export timeline` | Chronological events across all veterans |\n\n### Maintenance\n\n| Command | What it does |\n|---------|--------------|\n| `oralhistory stats` | Project statistics and coverage |\n| `oralhistory verify <id>` | Interactive wizard to fact-check a veteran |\n| `oralhistory reindex` | Rebuild search indexes |\n\n---\n\n## Transcript Formats\n\nThe system handles multiple formats:\n\n**Q:/A: format** (most reliable):\n```\nQ: Where were you when you heard about the attack?\n\nA: I was in the mess hall. Somebody came running in...\n```\n\n**JSON format** (for structured data):\n```json\n{\n  \"metadata\": {\"veteran_name\": \"John Rolfes\"},\n  \"turns\": [\n    {\"speaker\": \"interviewer\", \"text\": \"Where were you...\"},\n    {\"speaker\": \"veteran\", \"text\": \"I was in the mess hall...\"}\n  ]\n}\n```\n\n**Named speakers**:\n```\nINTERVIEWER: Where were you...\nROLFES: I was in the mess hall...\n```\n\n---\n\n## Project Structure\n\n```\nmybook/\n├── config.yaml          # Settings\n├── veterans/            # Parsed veteran data (regenerated on re-ingest)\n├── annotations/         # YOUR notes (preserved on re-ingest)\n├── index/               # Search database\n└── exports/             # Generated markdown\n```\n\n**Key insight:** `veterans/` can be regenerated. `annotations/` is your work. The system keeps them separate so you never lose your notes.\n\n---\n\n## Installation\n\n```bash\n# Clone and install\ngit clone https://github.com/r-huijts/writer.git\ncd writer\npip install -e .\n\n# Set up API keys (create .env file)\nPORTKEY_API_KEY=your_key\nPORTKEY_MODEL=claude-sonnet-4\nPORTKEY_EMBEDDING_MODEL=text-embedding-3-large\n```\n\n---\n\n## The Philosophy\n\nThis tool exists because oral history deserves better than Ctrl+F.\n\nThese veterans trusted someone with their memories. They sat down, often decades after the fact, and tried to put into words what it was like. Some of them are gone now. What remains is the transcript.\n\nYour job is to honor that by finding the truth in the noise, by connecting the fragments into a story, by making sure that when you quote someone, you understand the full context of what they said.\n\nThis tool helps you do that work. It doesn't write your book — it makes sure you've actually read your sources.\n\n---\n\n## License\n\nMIT\n\n---\n\n*Built for the Super Sixth project — a book about the 6th Armored Division in WWII.*\n","require_confirmation":true}},"jsonrpc":"2.0","id":63} { metadata: undefined }
